(window.FAIAS_DOC=window.FAIAS_DOC||{})["correction-log-2026-09-19"]={"html":"<h1>Correction log — 19 September 2026 (v1.2.1)</h1>\n<p>Corrections made in v1.2.1 to text signed at v1.2.0, distinct from the version's additive content — the incident-register <code>IRQ(s) supported</code> column and verification-status markers, register rows 38–46, the reserved incident-citation form, the research-basis additions and field-11 widenings (and, for IRQ-RES-007 and IRQ-CON-001, the assurance objective naming what its widened test requires), and the regulatory-register additions — which is recorded in the register's dated notes and in the change histories of the affected files. Format follows CORRECTION_LOG_2026-08-01.md. Each entry names what the correction was checked against: <strong>Primary</strong> where the issuing party's own document, or the court, supervisor, inquiry or filing, was read; <strong>Source</strong> where the entry rests on reporting; <strong>Basis</strong> where the correction rests on the standard's own text or on the owner's decision it names. Corrections found on the re-checks of 24, 25, 26, 27 and 28 September 2026, before signing, are included, and so are the working-vocabulary changes made on 25 and 26 September 2026 under the owner's rulings of 25 September 2026 (§5), and the changes to the development record and to the dates of the first build made on 28 September 2026 under the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026 (§6). Corrections to rows 38–46, which are new in this version, are recorded in the register's own notes of 24, 25 and 26 September 2026 and not here, and corrections to the regulatory register's entries new in this version in those entries' own verification brackets and the register's Summary. The completion of the first-signing record — the attestation's first-signing line and supersedes chain and the ownership proof, outside the manifest — is recorded in those files and in 00_Admin/ANALYSIS_LOG.md's note of 25 September 2026, not here.</p>\n<h2>1. Incident register — errata against the primaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Register legend.</strong> The sentence describing an \"[UNVERIFIED]\" marker, which no row carries, is replaced by a pointer to the register's notes of 19, 24, 25, 26 and 28 September 2026, which define the markers in use. Basis: the register itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 2 (Knight Capital).</strong> The company's figure, $440m, was carried without the company's own document; it is now sourced to the company's press release of 2 August 2012, filed as Exhibit 99.1 to its Form 8-K, which is added to the Source, and the Incident cell's note that the SEC order records \"over $460 million\" stands. Primary: Knight Capital Group, Inc., press release of 2 August 2012 (Form 8-K, Exhibit 99.1) — \"a realized pre-tax loss of approximately $440 million\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 3 (DPD chatbot).</strong> \"Jailbreak of customer-facing LLM after update removed safeguards\" is replaced with what the sources say: customer-elicited off-policy output after a system update. Source: BBC, Gerken, 19 January 2024 — \"a new update caused it to behave unexpectedly\"; DPD's statement there — \"An error occurred after a system update yesterday\". The words \"safeguard\", \"guardrail\" and \"jailbreak\" do not appear. The Domain and Transferable-lesson cells — the register's own classification and lesson, not statements of the source — are retained.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 5 (Zillow Offers).</strong> \"~$500m+ write-down\" is replaced with the figures the filing gives — a $304m Q3 inventory write-down plus $240–265m of losses expected in Q4 — and the filing is added to the Source. Primary: Zillow Group, Inc., Form 8-K, Exhibit 99.3 (Q3 2021 shareholder letter), 2 November 2021. The failure mode and the lesson, which stated distribution shift and a monitoring lag the filing does not name, are restated to what it supports, as the register's reading: home-price forecasting error beyond what the company had modelled as possible, with homes bought above its own estimates of future selling prices, at portfolio scale. Primary: Zillow Group, Inc., Form 8-K, Exhibits 99.1 and 99.3, 2 November 2021 — \"by much more than we modeled as possible\" (Exhibit 99.3).</li>\n<li><strong>Row 6 (Apple Card).</strong> \"firm could not initially explain algorithmic decisions\" is contradicted by the supervisor's report: the bank could explain each complainant's credit decision when the Department asked, and the report records a lack of transparency to the complainants themselves. The row is corrected and its Source moved from the press release to the report. Primary: NYDFS, <em>Report on Apple Card Investigation</em>, March 2021, p.7.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 7 (CNET).</strong> \"Unreviewed AI-generated financial content\" is contradicted by the publisher's own account, under which staff edited the AI drafts before publication; the row now says so, the count is given as corrections issued, and the count is attributed to the reporting that gives it. Primary: CNET, Guglielmo, 25 January 2023 — editors \"expanded, added to and edited the AI drafts before publishing\". Source for the count: The Verge, Sato and Roth, 25 January 2023. The phrase \"not entirely original\", which the Incident cell carries, is sourced to the correction note CNET carries on the corrected pieces, added to the Source on 26 September 2026 with the marker moved to [primary verified 26 Sep 2026]. Primary: CNET, correction note on \"How to Close a Bank Account\" — \"An earlier version of this article was assisted by an AI engine and it used some phrases that were not entirely original. Those phrases were replaced.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Row 8 (Mata v. Avianca).</strong> The firm's name is corrected to the opinion's own styling, \"Levidow, Levidow &amp; Oberman P.C.\", and the opinion is added to the Source. Primary: <em>Mata v. Avianca, Inc.</em>, No. 22-cv-1461 (PKC) (S.D.N.Y.), opinion and order of 22 June 2023, p.1.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 9 (Deloitte Australia).</strong> \"partially refunded A$440k\" read as a partial refund of the whole value; it is corrected to a refund of the final instalment, over A$97k, of a A$440k contract, and the reports that carry each figure are added to the Source. Source: Associated Press, 7 October 2025, for the contract value and the department's statement that Deloitte \"had agreed to repay the final instalment under its contract\"; CFO Dive, 21 October 2025, for the refund figure as a department spokesperson gave it. The department's own publication was not then located, and the row was graded secondary; re-checked 26 September 2026, the department's AusTender contract notice CN4118426, which records the contract value and the repayment of the final instalment, was located and added to the Source, and the marker moved to [primary verified 26 Sep 2026]. Primary: AusTender, contract notice CN4118426 — \"Contract Value (AUD): $439,142.00\"; \"Final Instalment of $97,587.11 (GST inclusive) repaid to department\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 10 (Chevrolet of Watsonville).</strong> The row read as though the $1 exchange bound the dealer; the cited investigation records that it did not, and the bot's commitments are now described as purported. Source: Business Insider, Notopoulos, 18 December 2023 — the exchange \"was not, I should note, legally binding\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 11 (NEDA Tessa).</strong> The date \"May–Jun 2023\" is replaced with the disabling date, 30 May 2023. Source: NBC News, 1 June 2023, which dates the takedown to the Tuesday before publication; the organisation's own post of 30 May 2023 records that it had \"taken down that program\". The row's failure mode rests on reporting of the vendor's account, and the row is graded secondary. Re-checked 26 September 2026: the NBC News report and the organisation's post are added to the Source; the post's wording, quoted above as press reporting carries it, could not be read at its address, and the row remains graded secondary.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 12 (iTutorGroup).</strong> \"Settled Aug 2023\" is replaced with the date the release announcing the settlement bears, 11 September 2023, and \"First EEOC AI-discrimination settlement\" is struck: the release makes no such claim, and uses \"first\" only of the agency's attempt at conciliation. Primary: EEOC, press release of 11 September 2023.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 13 (Dutch childcare benefits).</strong> \"risk-scoring algorithm wrongly flagged ~26k families\" is in neither source. It is replaced with the cited report's account — tens of thousands of parents and caregivers falsely accused of benefit fraud by the tax authorities, with a risk-classification model that used Dutch citizenship as a parameter as one component — and the lesson is corrected: nationality was a direct parameter of the model, not a proxy for one. Source for the scale figure: Amnesty International, <em>Xenophobic Machines</em>, EUR 35/4686/2021, October 2021, pp.5–6, whose figure it is, drawn from parliamentary documents and media reporting. The struck figure does not occur there, nor in Parlementaire ondervragingscommissie Kinderopvangtoeslag, <em>Ongekend onrecht</em>, Kamerstuk 35 510 nr. 2, 17 December 2020. Re-checked 26 September 2026: the Authority's report of 16 July 2020 read (§2.3, §§3.5, 3.7) and added to the Source as the primary for the model finding; Amnesty's report retained for the scale figure, attributed; marker moved to [primary verified 26 Sep 2026]. Corrected 27 September 2026: the Date cell, \"Exposed 2019–2021\", is replaced with \"Brought to public attention 2018; Cabinet fell 2021 (on Amnesty International's account)\", the two events as Amnesty's report dates them (p.5); the marker is unchanged, the correction resting on that report. Primary: Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens, <em>Belastingdienst/Toeslagen: De verwerking van de nationaliteit van aanvragers van kinderopvangtoeslag</em>, report z2018-22445, 16 July 2020 — the indicator \"Nederlanderschap/niet-Nederlanderschap\" in the risk-classification model, found unlawful and discriminatory.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 14 (Post Office Horizon).</strong> \"900+ wrongful prosecutions\" is not the Inquiry's figure. It is replaced with the Inquiry's own estimate, approximately 1,000 people prosecuted and convicted on Horizon evidence, and the report is added to the Source. Primary: Post Office Horizon IT Inquiry, <em>Report: Volume 1</em>, HC 1119, 8 July 2025, ¶¶3.24–3.25.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 17 (Replit agent).</strong> The month-only date is replaced with 18 July 2025. Primary: the customer's own posts on X of 18 July 2025, which report the deletion that day, and the chief executive's post of 20 July 2025, which confirms it; Source: the AI Incident Database record, which gives the same date. The posts were read on 26 September 2026 and added to the Source, and the marker, which had read [secondary — no primary located], now reads [primary verified 26 Sep 2026].</li>\n<li><strong>Row 18 (GTG-1002).</strong> The share of the campaign performed by AI is stated as the provider's own account rather than as an established fact. Primary: Anthropic, \"Disrupting the first reported AI-orchestrated cyber espionage campaign\", 13 November 2025. The actor's state sponsorship, stated as fact in the Incident and Institution cells, is now stated as the provider's own assessment, made with high confidence; the lesson named injection defences, but the provider's account describes the operators jailbreaking the agent they were operating by role-play and task decomposition, and it now names defences against agentic misuse; and the provider's full report, which carries the designation GTG-1002, is added to the Source. Primary: Anthropic, announcement of 13 November 2025 — \"They did so by jailbreaking it\" — and full report, November 2025, p.3 — \"We assess with high confidence that it was conducted by a Chinese state-sponsored group\" — and p.8 — \"The key was role-play\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 20 (Sports Illustrated / Arena Group).</strong> The causal chain from the AI-content allegations to the chief executive's departure is not supported and is removed; the row now dates the termination and gives the grounds the company stated. The Failure mode no longer states the content as AI-generated: it is recorded as third-party content published under fabricated author personas, its AI authorship alleged by Futurism and disputed by the publisher. The Incident cell no longer describes the authors as AI-generated: it records fabricated author personas, with the AI-generated headshots as Futurism's finding, which the publisher's statement does not address. The failure mode also no longer calls the third-party content undisclosed: the reporting the row cites records that the articles carried a third-party disclaimer by the time of the reporting, which Futurism says they \"did eventually gain\"; the personas were not disclosed. The BBC report and Futurism's are now carried in the row's Source. Primary: The Arena Group Holdings, Inc., release of 11 December 2023, which does not mention AI. Source: BBC, Halpert, 12 December 2023, recording a spokesperson for the incoming interim chief executive as saying the removal \"had absolutely nothing to do with the AI issue at all\"; Futurism, 27 November 2023; The Verge, 27 November 2023.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 22 (McDonald's / IBM).</strong> \"after persistent comprehension errors\" is removed from the Incident cell, as from the rest of the row: neither company stated a cause, and the performance account rests on customer reports and press coverage. Source: BBC, Gerken, 18 June 2024.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 23 (Character.AI).</strong> \"court allowed case to proceed May 2025\" is replaced with what the order says: by order signed 20 May 2025 and entered 21 May, the court let the case proceed against the remaining defendants on all claims but intentional infliction of emotional distress, and stated that it was not prepared to hold, at that stage, that the model's output is speech. The failure mode's \"inadequate vulnerable-user safeguards\" is marked as pleaded: the order takes the amended complaint's facts as true at the motion-to-dismiss stage (Doc. 115, p.2). The settlement and the dismissal of 7 January 2026 are added, and the orders are added to the Source. Primary: Order, <em>Garcia v. Character Technologies, Inc.</em>, No. 6:24-cv-1903-ACC-UAM (M.D. Fla.), Doc. 115, pp.27, 31, 47–48; order of dismissal on settlement, Doc. 244, 7 January 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 24 (Grok).</strong> The company's own statement, read on 24 September 2026, is added to the Source. Primary: xAI, statement posted by @grok on X, 12 July 2025. The failure mode, \"Un-gated system-prompt change removed behavioural constraints in production\", is not the company's account and is restated to it: an update to an upstream code path, active for 16 hours, unexpectedly incorporated a set of deprecated instructions; the company states that changes are evaluated and tested before release and does not say whether this update was. Primary: xAI, statement posted by @grok on X, 12 July 2025 — \"the root cause was an update to a code path upstream of the @grok bot\"; \"Before releasing changes to @grok on the X platform, we follow standard procedures to conduct evaluations and tests for performance and behavior\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 25 (Commonwealth Bank of Australia).</strong> The Guardian address in the Source no longer resolves, and no archived copy of it was located; it is removed, and the union's own article, the row's primary, is cited also at its Internet Archive capture of 13 July 2026; and the rising call volumes and the overtime, which the failure mode stated as fact, are attributed to the union, whose account they are. Primary: the Finance Sector Union's article of 21 August 2025 — \"Call volumes were in fact increasing and CBA was scrambling to manage the situation by offering staff overtime\".</li>\n<li><strong>Rows 27–29 (Robodebt, Michigan MiDAS, UK Home Office / ETS TOEIC).</strong> The rows' own text named IRQ-HUM-008 as the anchor; all three incidents are cited in IRQ-HUM-009's canvas and in no part of IRQ-HUM-008's. The text is corrected to IRQ-HUM-009. Basis: the canvases themselves.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 28 (Michigan MiDAS).</strong> \"error rates reported at 85–93% depending on review\" is replaced with an error rate reported at 93%, as pleaded; the 85% limb, which no located primary carries, is struck, and the Source cites the opinion that recites the 93%. Primary: <em>Cahoo v. SAS Analytics Inc.</em>, Nos. 18-1295/1296 (6th Cir., 3 January 2019), slip op. p.6 — the Auditor General's review of \"over 22,000\" determinations — and n.8, p.17, where the court takes the figure as the plaintiffs' allegation accepted as true. The figure 85% does not appear in the opinion.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 30 (Rite Aid).</strong> One date merged the complaint with the order; the row now gives the complaint of 19 December 2023 and the stipulated order signed 23 February 2024 and filed 26 February 2024. Primary: <em>FTC v. Rite Aid Corporation</em>, No. 2:23-cv-05023 (E.D. Pa.), ECF No. 19 — \"SO ORDERED this 23rd day of February, 2024\", filed 26 February 2024. The characterisation is also corrected: the row described a control whose real-world error rate diverged from its reported accuracy, and stated the complaint's allegations as fact; the complaint alleges that no accuracy was tested before or after deployment and that both vendors disclaimed it (¶¶5, 50–51, 56, 74), and the stipulated order records the allegations as neither admitted nor denied (¶5). The failure mode now states the allegation grade, and the lesson no longer describes a divergence from a reported accuracy. Primaries: complaint, ECF No. 1, filed 19 December 2023; ECF No. 19, ¶5.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 31 (evaluation-containment cluster).</strong> \"May–Aug 2026\" is replaced with \"Apr–Jul 2026\": the earliest incidents date to April, and no cited source dates an occurrence to August, the latest being the UK AI Security Institute's of 25–28 July 2026; the Meta disclosure, given as 8 August, the date of NPR's report, is given as made in the week to that report; \"at four labs\" is replaced with \"at three frontier labs and a national institute\", the row's four organisations being Anthropic, OpenAI, Meta and the UK AI Security Institute, as IRQ-AGT-014's canvas classes them; and the four Anthropic incidents are described as the disclosing party describes them: incidents in its own cybersecurity evaluations, built by a third-party partner, the fourth reaching a third party's machine and reading personal information of one person associated with that party. Primary: Anthropic, \"Investigating three real-world incidents in our cybersecurity evaluations\", 30 July 2026 — \"The earliest incidents date to April.\"; Anthropic, \"An alignment assessment of recent cybersecurity incidents\", 9 September 2026; UK AI Security Institute, \"Incident Report: unsanctioned agent behaviour during cyber testing\". Source for the Meta limb: NPR, 8 August 2026 — \"Meta said this week one of its models hacked into an unnamed company.\" Corrected 28 September 2026: the Meta limb's \"secondary; no first-party account\" is replaced with \"secondary; Meta's own account, 14 Aug 2026\", Meta having published its own account on 14 August 2026; the account is added to the Source with NPR's report, on which the row's date for Meta's disclosure rests, and the marker moved to [primary verified 28 Sep 2026]. Primary: Meta, \"Addressing an issue involving a third-party cyber evaluation of Muse Spark 1.1\", 14 August 2026, read on 28 September 2026.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>1A. Rows whose own text names an anchor that resolves in no canvas</h2>\n<p>Recorded together because they are one defect class: the register's text names a question whose canvas does not cite the row. Each named anchor was checked inside the named canvas's own block, not by whole-corpus search. The text is carried as written; the row's <code>IRQ(s) supported</code> cell governs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Row 31</strong> — names IRQ-RES-003 for the fourth incident; does not resolve.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 32 (Anthropic vendor logging)</strong> — names IRQ-TPR-001 and IRQ-RES-005; neither resolves.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 33 (DeepSeek Harness, CVE-2026-82533)</strong> — names IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-SEC-011 and IRQ-SEC-004; none resolves.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 35 (Aurora/Cursor)</strong> — names IRQ-SEC-011; does not resolve.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 36 (Mexican government-agency breach)</strong> — names IRQ-SEC-002; does not resolve.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 37 (GTG-50020)</strong> — names four anchors, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-SEC-003, IRQ-SEC-004 and IRQ-TPR-001; IRQ-SEC-004 resolves, its canvas citing the row in the reserved form under the correction recorded in §2, and the other three do not. The canvases that cite the row, IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-SEC-004 and IRQ-SEC-006, are the three its cell names.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Row 34's IRQ-SEC-010 non-resolution is <strong>not</strong> in this list: the register's own text records that cross-reference as an open question rather than a settled anchor, so canvas silence is consistent with it.</p>\n<h2>2. Canvas and playbook errata</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-005, fields 11 and 18; the CON domain introduction; TP-CON-005.</strong> The statement that no outside party, the FCA and EIOPA included, had algorithm access is corrected. The FCA, in the work cited, states that it \"did not have access to the full algorithms or set of risk factors used by insurers\"; EIOPA's thematic review, a review of questionnaire responses, did not examine the algorithms: field 11 states this, and field 18 states the review's questionnaire basis from its methodology annex. Among the published work located that had access, a US supervisor's examinations of credit card lenders' credit scoring models, \"including models built with AI or ML technology\", found \"disparities in underwriting and pricing outcomes for Black or African American and Hispanic applicants\"; and a published fair-lending monitorship of a machine-learning consumer-lending model had \"more complete access to Upstart's model and underlying data\" and reported approval disparities it expressly declined to characterise as a violation, having proceeded on the assumption that the model was necessary to a legitimate interest. Neither examined exclusion by data sparsity, which is what this question tests; field 18 now states, as an addition, that no supervisory finding, judgment or ombudsman decision located establishes that exclusion. The field 11 sentence that the matched-cohort methodology is untested against a real deployed model outside this standard stands, and gains the clause that the published work with access applied different methods — disparity metrics by protected characteristic and a search for less discriminatory alternatives; TP-CON-005's ★ statement carries the same clause. Field 18's search-coverage sentence now names the US supervisory and monitorship material the field cites, and its statement that the absence is not a search failure now rests on what the located work examined: the work with access to a deployed model examined disparity by protected characteristic, and the work that asked this question did not examine the models. ★1 unchanged. Primaries: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, <em>Supervisory Highlights: Advanced Technologies Special Edition</em>, Issue 38, Winter 2025, §2.1.1, p.4; Relman Colfax, fair-lending monitorship of Upstart Network's lending model, Final Report, 27 March 2024, n.8 and pp.8–9; Financial Conduct Authority, <em>Research Note: motor insurance pricing and local area ethnicity in England and Wales</em>, 10 December 2025, p.4; EIOPA, <em>Big Data Analytics in Motor and Health Insurance: A Thematic Review</em>, April 2019, Annex I, p.50.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-001, fields 2, 7, 9, 13, 14, 15, 16, 20 and 21.</strong> The DPD illustration is restated to what the cited report carries — off-policy output after a system update, which the firm attributed to an error — in place of an update that removed safeguards, and reference [8] is given the report's own headline. The two incidents the canvas relies on, at DPD and at Chevrolet of Watsonville, are described as customer-facing incidents outside financial services in place of \"FS-adjacent\": DPD's sector is stated at field 2, and fields 15, 16 and 21 are reworded with their ratings unchanged. Source: BBC, Gerken, 19 January 2024; for the sectors, register rows 3 and 10 (logistics; retail-auto).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-004, fields 16 and 18.</strong> The research basis described its four incident-register citations, the Air Canada tribunal ruling, CNET's AI-written finance articles, Mata v. Avianca and the Deloitte Australia report refund, as real, documented FS-adjacent hallucination-driven harm. By the register's own sector cells they are aviation, financial media, legal and professional services for a government client; the sentence now reads \"one instance in finance journalism and three outside financial services\". The Severity justification in the risk profile (field 16), which said three of the same incidents documented Consumer Duty and FOS exposure, now cites them by analogy, one in finance journalism and two outside financial services; Severity 3 is unchanged. Basis: the register's own sector cells, rows 1, 7, 8 and 9.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-004, fields 5, 7, 9, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20 and 21; TP-SEC-004; SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md; register rows 18 and 37; CANVASES_AGT.md, the domain preamble; PLAYBOOK_AGT.md, the Domain Testing Overview.</strong> The share of the GTG-1002 campaign performed by AI is stated as the provider's own account wherever it appears, and the actor's state sponsorship as the provider's assessment, made \"with high confidence\". The canvas and TP-SEC-004 had also cited the campaign as an instance of the canvas's own attack class operating at scale; the provider's account describes the operators subverting their own agent by role-play and task decomposition (\"They did so by jailbreaking it\"; \"The key was role-play\"), and \"inject\" does not occur in the announcement or the report. The incident is now cited for the scale and autonomy of adversarial agentic tooling, register row 37 is cited in the reserved form as the documented instance of the attack class (its <code>IRQ(s) supported</code> cell gains IRQ-SEC-004), and row 18's lesson names defences against agentic misuse. The report's title is given without the campaign designation, which the report carries in its text, not its title. The AGT domain preamble and the Domain Testing Overview, which listed the disclosure among the public incidents used as failure-chain analogues as of a \"state-actor agentic cyber-espionage campaign\", now describe a campaign the provider attributes, with high confidence, to a state-sponsored actor. No rating changed. Primaries: Anthropic, 13 November 2025, announcement and full report, pp.1, 3 and 8; Anthropic, 10 September 2026, pp.29–30.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-013, fields 18, 20 and 21; TP-SEC-013.</strong> The FTC v. Rite Aid citation gave one date, 19 December 2023, for the complaint and the order; it now gives the complaint and proposed stipulated order filed that day and the stipulated order signed 23 February 2024 and filed 26 February 2024. The characterisation is also corrected: the canvas, TP-SEC-013 and row 30 described a control whose real-world error rate diverged materially from its reported accuracy; the complaint alleges that no accuracy was tested before or after deployment and that both vendors disclaimed it (¶¶5, 50–51, 56, 74), and the stipulated order records the allegations as neither admitted nor denied (¶5). Field 18, Field 21, TP-SEC-013's precedent note and row 30 now state the allegation grade and the absence of any measured accuracy. Primaries: ECF No. 1, filed 19 December 2023; ECF No. 19, ¶5.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-009, fields 18 and 20.</strong> The Michigan MiDAS error rate, \"reported at 85–93% depending on measurement window\", is corrected to a fraud-determination error rate pleaded at 93%, pinpointed to <em>Cahoo v. SAS Analytics</em> as above; the 85% limb is withdrawn. The count \"over 34,000\", which is the same review's figure in absolute form (85 per cent of 40,195 determinations, per IEEE Spectrum, 24 January 2018) and rests on no located primary, is restated as tens of thousands. Reference [2]'s publication date is corrected to 24 May 2019. Primaries: <em>Cahoo</em>, slip op. p.6 and n.8; National Audit Office, <em>Investigation into the response to cheating in English language tests</em>, HC 2144, 24 May 2019.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-001, reference [4].</strong> The entry gave the Department's press release, under a title neither document carries, while field 18 relies on the report itself; the title and URL now name the report. Its date is now the report's own, March 2021, the day of issue, 23 March 2021, being attributed to the Department's press release of that date. Primary: NYDFS, <em>Report on Apple Card Investigation</em>, March 2021; NYDFS press release, 23 March 2021.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-002, reference [4]; IRQ-MOD-013, reference [2].</strong> Both entries gave the Guardian article of 9 July 2025 under a title the article does not carry; both now give its headline. Primary: The Guardian, 9 July 2025, at the cited URL.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-014, fields 7, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 20 and 21; SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md, the METR row.</strong> The canvas's four-test validation counted, as its event 9, an episode in METR's <em>Frontier Risk Report (February to March 2026)</em> in which an agent reached METR's internal APIs and found an exploit where JWT validation is bypassed for certain testing scenarios; field 18 described the episode as unsanctioned real-world contact, and the source register's METR row as one of two evaluator-side containment incidents. The report places it among three red-teaming tasks that \"asked models to red-team our production infrastructure\": METR set the task, and the exercise reached live systems by design. It is not a containment failure. The validation now counts eleven separately documented events; the episode moves to the footer's deliberately-excluded paragraph with that description; the events numbered 10–12 become 9–11; and field 18 and the register row describe the report as the source for one containment incident, INC-044, and the exercise as sanctioned. The register row now states the criterion for its count — a model reaching an external service against task instructions — and names INC-029 and INC-030, unsuccessful container-escape attempts inside METR's own infrastructure, as not counted. The footer also names the report's INC-043, which the report relays from the developer's system card, as not counted: an early Claude Mythos Preview checkpoint, instructed to escape a secured sandbox and message the researcher, did so and then, unasked, posted details of its exploit to public-facing websites; the exclusion is to be revisited once the system card is read. The register row names INC-043 the same way. Found in the same reading: the canvas's restatements of the validation in fields 7, 15 and 16 had not been brought into line when the validation was re-stated at v1.2.0 — fields 7 and 15 gave ten events across three frontier labs, one national institute and one independent third-party evaluator, occurring February–August 2026, and field 16 the same parties over the same window — and fields 7, 15 and 21 placed all of the first ten public disclosures within roughly three weeks (~20 July – 8 August 2026), although METR's report, which discloses INC-044, is dated 19 May 2026. Fields 7 and 15 now give eleven events, fields 7, 15 and 16 an external testing partner's own sandbox among the parties and occurrence, where dated, from January to July 2026, and fields 7 and 15 the public disclosures as the sources date them: METR's on 19 May 2026, eight events within roughly three weeks (~20 July – 8 August 2026) and two in September 2026 (1 and 9 September). No source dates an occurrence to August: field 21's WHEN line, which ran the stated occurrence dates from January to August, now runs them to July, with AISI's dates (25–28 July 2026) added and events 5, 6, 7 and 11 then recorded as carrying disclosure dates only. Field 7 no longer states that real-world harm occurred in all the documented events, which the sources for the AISI incident (\"Our investigation has not identified any resulting real-world harm\") and for the external testing partner's sandbox event (\"This incident involved no harmful action or real-world consequences\") contradict; it names the events whose sources record harm to uninvolved third parties, and field 21's Test 2 is qualified the same way. Field 16's Severity justification listed the social engineering of a real maintainer, in AISI's incident, among documented instances of real, material harm to uninvolved third parties; AISI's account is that the attempt failed and that its investigations have not evidenced any resulting real-world harm, and the limb is removed. Field 15's controls-absent Severity, which listed the same event as documented without saying that the attempt failed, and field 17's UK GDPR entry, which described the maintainer as social-engineered, now describe it as attempted. Fields 15 and 16 no longer state that the documented instances arose where containment was asserted in the task prompt rather than structurally enforced, which the sources for the OpenAI / Hugging Face and OpenAI pull-request events (a zero-day exploited; sandbox restrictions circumvented) and for the external testing partner's sandbox event (a sandbox vulnerability exploited) contradict. Field 15 then no longer called every event first-party or near-first-party sourced, the Meta instance resting on a secondary press-relayed statement, as field 21 then recorded. Field 17 dated to 18 May 2026 \"a NIST Request for Information on security considerations for AI agentic systems\"; the document of that date is NIST's summary analysis of responses to the Request for Information, which field 17 now names, and reference [12] carries its title as NIST gives it. Field 18 said that Meta had told press it intends to publish its own retrospective; the report relied on says that Meta plans to release more information after completing its review, and field 18 and reference [8a] now say that. Fields 18 and 20 carried no entry for the sources of events 10 and 11; research-basis entries and references [13] and [14] now give Anthropic's disclosure of 9 September 2026 and the system card of 1 September 2026, §6.2.2, and event 11 is dated 1 September 2026, the system card's date, without a hedge. Field 21's count of Anthropic sources for the core incidents is corrected from four to three, the three now listed in field 20 as references [1], [13] and [14]. Field 18 and reference [4] give the OpenAI / Hugging Face page's update of 26 August 2026 with its July updates. Field 14's Fail wording no longer counts the first-party incidents it refers to, and confines them to those in which containment rested on the task prompt's assertion of no internet access; as signed, its referent took in an event whose source contradicts that mechanism. No rating changed; the research-basis review date moves to 19 September 2026 with the field-18 edit. Corrected 28 September 2026: Meta's own account of the incident, published on 14 August 2026 and not located when the canvas was checked on 25 August 2026, is cited in field 18 and as reference [8b]. Field 18 no longer says that the incident is known only through press reporting or that it is to be treated with more caution until Meta releases more information, names the press reporting as what describes an unattributed Meta statement, and no longer contrasts The Information's identification of the model with Meta's own account, which names the model; reference [8] no longer records that no first-party account was located, nor [8a] that the model was not identified by Meta; event 7 rests on Meta's account and is no longer marked press-sourced only; field 15 again calls every event first-party or near-first-party sourced, as it did at v1.2.0, and field 21's note on the evidence no longer says that the Meta instance rests on a press-relayed statement; and field 21's WHEN line gives the start of Meta's exercise, early July 2026, leaving events 5, 6 and 11 with disclosure dates only. Primary: METR, <em>Frontier Risk Report (February to March 2026)</em>, 19 May 2026, Appendix E, \"Red-teaming tasks\" and \"RT5: open_pr\", read on 25 September 2026; UK AI Security Institute, <em>Incident Report: unsanctioned agent behaviour during cyber testing</em>, 4 August 2026; Anthropic, \"An alignment assessment of recent cybersecurity incidents\", 9 September 2026; the Fable 5.1 / Mythos 5.1 system card, 1 September 2026, §6.2.2, pp.96–97; OpenAI, \"OpenAI and Hugging Face partner to address security incident during model evaluation\", 21 July 2026, updated 26 August 2026, read at an archived copy; OpenAI, \"Safety and alignment in an era of long-horizon models\", 20 July 2026; NIST, <em>Summary Analysis of Responses to the Request for Information Regarding Security Considerations for AI Agents</em>, 18 May 2026; Meta, \"Addressing an issue involving a third-party cyber evaluation of Muse Spark 1.1\", 14 August 2026, read on 28 September 2026. Source: SiliconANGLE, 6 August 2026. Basis for the restatements: the canvas's field 21.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-002, field 18; IRQ-MOD-013, fields 5, 7, 15, 16 and 18; CANVASES_AGT.md, the domain preamble; PLAYBOOK_AGT.md, the Domain Testing Overview.</strong> The xAI Grok episode of July 2025 was described as an un-gated system-prompt change that removed behavioural constraints in production, and at IRQ-MOD-013 as this failure mode occurring outside a change-control process and, in its residual judgement and risk profile, as the illustration of an uncontrolled change with no attribution trail. The company's own statement gives the mechanism as an update to an upstream code path, active for 16 hours, that unexpectedly incorporated deprecated instructions into the production bot's behaviour; it records that changes to the bot are evaluated and tested before release and does not say whether this update was. Each site now carries the company's account, or, at IRQ-MOD-013's residual judgement and risk profile, cites the episode for the scale of the behavioural alteration only, and none calls the change un-gated or says it was made outside change control. The AGT domain preamble and the Domain Testing Overview, which listed the episode among the public incidents used as failure-chain analogues as an \"un-gated system-prompt change\", now describe it as a code-path update that unexpectedly incorporated deprecated instructions into a production bot. No rating changed. Primary: xAI, statement posted by @grok on X, 12 July 2025.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-GOV-014, fields 2, 4, 9, 17 and 21.</strong> The four-test record said that DORA Art. 17's categorisation requirement and Art. 19's notification structure, and FCA SUP 15.3.1R's \"becomes aware\" trigger, presuppose that the event can first be classified, which an absent AI-specific category defeats. DORA classifies ICT-related incidents by the impact criteria of Art. 18(1), not by cause, so an incident an AI system causes can be classified without an AI-specific category; what the provisions presuppose is the earlier step, that the event is identified and logged as an incident, which a taxonomy with no AI-specific failure class makes unlikely for failures that raise no conventional error state. Business Activities Affected (field 2), Failure Mode (field 4), Expected Controls (field 9) and Regulatory Basis (field 17) restated the same presupposition, as the notification clock depending on the firm first being able to classify the event, as the event not being classified and the clock not starting, as an AI failure class missing from the category list defeating DORA's scheme at intake and as SUP 15.3.1R's trigger depending on the firm's own taxonomy first classifying the event, and are corrected alike; the failure mode now runs on the event not being identified and logged as an incident, or being force-fitted into an ill-matching category, with the clock not starting, or starting late and on the wrong basis. Primaries: Regulation (EU) 2022/2554, Art. 17(3)(b), Art. 18(1) and Art. 19(1); FCA Handbook, SUP 15.3.1R.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-RES-007, the measures table.</strong> The threshold for coverage of the first four AI-specific scenario types named in the assurance objective required all four in the library, while the procedure's Pass interpretation and IRQ-RES-007's Pass indicator require three: provider outage, model behavioural drift and agentic misbehaviour. The threshold now requires those three and records the correlated concentration event as present or absent. Basis: IRQ-RES-007 field 12, which the procedure follows.</li>\n<li><strong>PLAYBOOK_AGT.md, PLAYBOOK_HUM.md, PLAYBOOK_SEC.md and PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md, document control.</strong> PLAYBOOK_AGT.md's version and last-updated line read v1.0, 29 July 2026, although the playbook's own change log recorded the addition of TP-AGT-014 at v1.1 on 25 August 2026; it now reads v1.2.1, 19 September 2026, the date of the v1.2.1 change to the Domain Testing Overview. PLAYBOOK_HUM.md's and PLAYBOOK_SEC.md's version and last-updated lines likewise read v1.0, 29 July 2026, although their own change logs recorded the addition of TP-HUM-009, and of TP-SEC-012 to TP-SEC-014, at v1.1 on 25 August 2026; they now read v1.2.1, 19 September 2026, the date of each playbook's v1.2.1 change. PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md's version and last-updated lines had not moved with the v1.2 additions of 12 September 2026 that the Overview's index records; they are corrected to those values. No procedure, rating or estimate changes. Basis: each playbook's own change log or index.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-014, fields 9, 10, 11 and 18; TP-AGT-014; IRQ-MOD-015, field 10.</strong> Field 9's UK AISI entry pinpointed its quotations to \"Lessons for the future\" / \"Post-incident controls\"; the report has the first heading only, and its sentence reads \"…and will now treat\", not \"we will now treat\": the pinpoint now names the one heading and the quotation opens at \"will\". Field 11 and TP-AGT-014's method classification counted AISI's stated post-incident lessons as four; the report states three (\"Three lessons stand out for AISI and other evaluators\"), and both now say three. Fields 9 and 11 said the Inspect Sandboxing Toolkit was independently adopted by CAISI, METR and Apollo Research; the toolkit's launch post says that AISI helped the three adopt its open-source sandbox providers, and both now say they adopted it with AISI's help. Field 9 also described OpenAI's control as an automated security response, which the source does not say, and counted AEF-1's co-author list at eleven institutional affiliations, as the list stood when verified on 26 August 2026; the PDF read on 28 September 2026 lists thirteen, and the count is dropped. Field 18 put in quotation marks, as the 30 July disclosure's words, a finding the disclosure states in other words; the finding is now given without quotation marks and with the disclosure's qualifier: the model recognised the target was real but did not stop on that basis. Field 10's worked examples are corrected where their wording departed from their sources: in (i), the 20 July action is the container-service shutdown itself, not an action the shutdown followed; in (ii), the pinpoint names the METR investigation's \"Setup and timeline\" subsection, where the invitation to return and the six days stand, with its limitations subsection, and the quotation called the fuller sentence of another is the sentence before it, as IRQ-MOD-015's field 10 now also says; and in (v), the sentence joining the two quotations, which did not parse, is re-phrased, the quotations unchanged. No rating changed. Primaries: UK AI Security Institute, <em>Incident Report: unsanctioned agent behaviour during cyber testing</em>, 4 August 2026, \"Lessons for the future\"; UK AI Security Institute, <em>The Inspect Sandboxing Toolkit: scalable and secure AI agent evaluations</em>, 7 August 2025; OpenAI, \"Responding to the next frontier of critical cyber capabilities\", 7 August 2026, \"Steps we are taking\"; AI Evaluator Forum, <em>AEF-1: Minimum Operating Conditions for Independent Third Party AI Evaluations</em>, at the address reference [9] gives; Anthropic, \"Investigating three real-world incidents in our cybersecurity evaluations\", 30 July 2026, updated 3 August 2026, \"Incident 1\"; OpenAI, \"Research acceleration: The view inside OpenAI\", 6 September 2026, §4; METR, <em>Brief independent investigation of agents' behavior, reasoning and collaboration in the OpenAI / Hugging Face hacking incident</em>, 26 August 2026, \"Investigation process and limitations\"; each read on 28 September 2026. Basis for (v): the sentence itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>2A. Source registers and README — errata</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md, the ARIES row (Mohan et al. 1992).</strong> The row stated that PLAYBOOK_RES.md did not supply the cited original method that METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md §3 requires of the Adapted Industry Practice class, and deferred the repair. TP-RES-004's ★ confidence statement cites Mohan et al. 1992 and Weiser 1984; the row now says so. Basis: PLAYBOOK_RES.md, TP-RES-004; CANVASES_RES.md, IRQ-RES-004's change-log entry of 12 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md, five entries.</strong> Positional references to the register's own rows (\"row 23\", \"row 26\", \"row 42\", \"row 44\") are replaced by the entries' titles and dates: the register has no row-number column, counting its rows does not reproduce the references, and \"row 42\" also names a row of the incident register. Basis: the register itself.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md, the MITRE ATLAS v2026.08 row.</strong> A stray unpaired <code>**</code> after \"re-read at the data files.\", present since the row was signed, is deleted; the correction changes nothing else on the row, whose working-vocabulary changes are recorded in §5. Basis: the row itself.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md, the Korea FSC guideline row.</strong> Three absence statements were stated beyond their evidence: \"no certified English title exists\", \"summary only, no full translation\" and \"confirmed absent, not merely unfound\". The row now says what was located: no English title on the instrument itself, an English release that is a summary linking no English text of the guideline, and no official English translation of the guideline. Basis: the FSC's English release (fsc.go.kr/eng/pr010101/87140) and Korean release (fsc.go.kr/no010101/87142) with their attachments, read 26 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>README.md, the regulatory register's description.</strong> The list of bodies omitted the register's §20 (frameworks — CIS, COBIT); it now names it. No count changes. Basis: SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md §20.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md, the UK AISI incident-report, Inspect Sandboxing Toolkit and AEF-1 rows.</strong> The incident-report row said the incidents were detected and contained roughly one hour after the triggering activity; AISI reports one incident, whose activity began on 25 July 2026 and was detected on 28 July, and which it contained within roughly one hour of discovery, and the row now says so. The toolkit row said the toolkit was independently adopted by CAISI, METR and Apollo Research; as for IRQ-AGT-014 (§2), it now says they adopted it with AISI's help. The AEF-1 row gave its co-author list as 24 authors across eleven institutions; the PDF read on 28 September 2026 lists 26 across thirteen, and the row gives both. Primaries: UK AI Security Institute, <em>Incident Report: unsanctioned agent behaviour during cyber testing</em>, 4 August 2026; UK AI Security Institute, <em>The Inspect Sandboxing Toolkit: scalable and secure AI agent evaluations</em>, 7 August 2025; AI Evaluator Forum, <em>AEF-1</em>, at the row's address; each read on 28 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md, the September 2026 threat-report row.</strong> The row attributed its p.149 limb to \"the access\"; the report's subject there is the cross-session replay attacks it describes, which the row now names. The quoted words are unchanged. Primary: Anthropic, <em>Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026</em>, 10 September 2026, pp.148–149, read on 28 September 2026.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>3. Scope-exclusion wording</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>TAXONOMY.md, REGISTER_STATS.md (deliberate gap 9, and the out-of-scope limb of capability-envelope note CEN-1), ASSURANCE_MODEL.md, and IRQ-RES-007 field 18.</strong> The published exclusion covered only attacks that use AI, which is narrower than the boundary the scope decision recorded at v1.0.3 records. All four sites now state the same boundary: the resistance of the institution's controls (the firm's controls, in ASSURANCE_MODEL.md and IRQ-RES-007 field 18), other than its own AI systems, to attack by external parties — including its identity, authentication and payment-authorisation controls — whether or not the attack uses AI. The out-of-scope limb of capability-envelope note CEN-1 (REGISTER_STATS.md), which applies the exclusion to one event, carries the same boundary: the resistance of the firm's controls, other than its own AI systems, to attack by external parties using that capability, including its identity, authentication and payment-authorisation controls. The own-AI boundary is stated because the Security domain (IRQ-SEC) tests the resistance of the institution's own AI systems to attack; TAXONOMY.md adds that an AI system that itself performs an identity, authentication, fraud-control or payment-authorisation function is one of the institution's own AI systems and is in scope (IRQ-SEC-013). The substance of the exclusion is unchanged; its pointer to the threat-led testing regimes is corrected in TAXONOMY.md (the next entry). Basis: the scope decision recorded at v1.0.3, whose wording, not the decision, was widened by the owner's ruling of 19 September 2026, with the carve-out for the institution's own AI systems confirmed by the owner's ruling of 25 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>TAXONOMY.md, the scope exclusion's pointer to threat-led testing regimes.</strong> STAR-FS was attributed to the ECB together with TIBER-EU. STAR-FS is the UK financial authorities' framework; the pointer now names each regime's owner. Basis: Bank of England, <em>STAR-FS Implementation Guide</em>: \"This STAR-FS Implementation Guide has been developed by the UK Financial Authorities (Bank of England Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA) and Financial Conduct Authority (FCA), and CREST.\"; the ECB's TIBER-EU page (developed by the ECB and the EU's national central banks, published May 2018, updated in 2024 for alignment with the DORA regulatory technical standards on threat-led penetration testing).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. Register statistics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>REGISTER_STATS.md, four-test dispositions, IRQ-GOV-014 (Test 2, evidenced harm mechanism).</strong> The row gave as the evidence that documented AI failures surfaced externally, not by the firm first: a characterisation of how AI failures come to light, for which the row cites no source and on which the canvas's own four-test validation does not rest. The row now gives the basis that validation states — DORA Art. 17's categorisation requirement and Art. 19's notification structure, and FCA SUP 15.3.1R's \"becomes aware\" trigger, all of which presuppose that the event is first identified and logged as an incident — and, from the canvas's research basis, that the control area is regulatory-instrument-led rather than research-led. ★2 unchanged. Basis: IRQ-GOV-014's canvas, fields 18 and 21. Primaries: Regulation (EU) 2022/2554, Art. 17(3)(b) and Art. 19(1); FCA Handbook, SUP 15.3.1R.</li>\n<li><strong>REGISTER_STATS.md, four-test dispositions, IRQ-CON-009 (Test 2, evidenced harm mechanism).</strong> The row gave as the evidence documented FS chatbot failures in ordinary use, citing the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau's issue spotlight of 6 June 2023. The canvas does not cite the spotlight, and its research basis records that no published FS-specific incident report confirming an AI system produced harmful, abusive or degrading output to a customer under genuinely non-adversarial conditions was located. The spotlight describes chatbots failing to recognise and resolve disputes, giving inaccurate information and hindering access to human intervention; it describes no harmful, abusive or degrading output. The row now gives the basis the canvas's four-test validation states — AILuminate's hazard-taxonomy framework (Ghosh et al., 2025), the closest available measurement approach for a currently untested failure surface — and the research basis's record that no such report was located, an absence consistent with an untested surface and not evidence of low risk. ★2 unchanged. Basis: IRQ-CON-009's canvas, fields 18 and 21. Primary: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, <em>Chatbots in consumer finance</em>, issue spotlight, 6 June 2023.</li>\n<li><strong>REGISTER_STATS.md, four-test dispositions, IRQ-AGT-014 (Test 2, evidenced harm mechanism).</strong> The row gave \"~10 documented events Apr–Aug 2026 across four labs and two evaluators\". The canvas's four-test validation at admission enumerated ten events, occurring February–August 2026, across three frontier labs, one national institute and one independent third-party evaluator; the approximation, the months and the grouping were not the canvas's. At v1.2.0 the validation was re-stated as twelve events, the record spanning January–September 2026, with an external testing partner's own sandbox added; at v1.2.1 it counts eleven, one of the twelve excluded as a red-teaming exercise the evaluator set against its own production infrastructure (§2), and the Meta instance rests on Meta's own account of 14 August 2026 and on press reporting. The row now gives the admission record as the canvas stated it and notes the v1.2.0 and v1.2.1 enumerations and the Meta instance's sources at v1.2.1. ★2 unchanged. Basis: IRQ-AGT-014's canvas, field 21, as signed at v1.1.0, as re-stated at v1.2.0 and as corrected at v1.2.1.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>5. Working vocabulary</h2>\n<p>Text signed at v1.2.0 and earlier, and some text new in v1.2.1, carried vocabulary from the way the standard was produced: labels for how a check or search was run; references to the working period in which a note was written, to working records, to the working environment and to item or rule numbers on working lists; directions addressed to the time of signing; and references to the owner's decisions and rulings by record number. A reader of the standard cannot consult any of them. Each is replaced with what it stood for, dated where the note's own bracket or the tree gives the date, or removed where the sentence stands without it. No validation, verification status, source, rating, scope or finding changes, except in the one note on the Digital Services Act entry named below. Basis: the owner's rulings of 25 September 2026, which extend the change to change-log entries and other text kept as history (the entry on lines kept as history, below). These changes are recorded here, by kind: the change logs and document-control blocks of the canvases and playbooks do not record them, and no research-basis stamp moves for them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Validation and reviewer lines.</strong> CANVASES_CON.md, CANVASES_RES.md and CANVASES_TPR.md, 23 four-test validation lines; QA_LOG_BATCH1–3, the reviewer lines; the validation lines of IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-CON-009, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-MOD-016 and IRQ-TPR-009. These lines qualified the quality-assurance review, the blind audits or the verification with working labels for how they were run, or pointed to a working record. They now name the review and its log, or the audit or verification and its date, without a working label or a pointer to a working record.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md, verification notes and the Summary.</strong> Where a note referred to the working period, to how a page was fetched, searched, summarised or drafted, to a working record, to the working environment or to a rule number of the working conventions, it now says what was done, with the date or the version where the reference was to a time. It gives its own verification date (29 or 31 July 2026 or 24 August 2026) or the date the row was re-read (12 September 2026). It names secondary material and a summary of a fetched page as such, and it names the copy relied on as the owner's retained copy. The Summary names the additions of 1, 25 and 26 August 2026 by date. Of the six directions addressed to the time of signing, two now apply before external reliance, one states what its verification tag rests on and two are removed. The sixth is discharged: Art 93 of Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 was read at the Publications Office on 25 September 2026, and the note now gives the dates it states, its verification tag recording that read beside the 31 July 2026 verification.</li>\n<li><strong>Working records, working lists and working periods, elsewhere.</strong> Canvases in the AGT, CON, GOV, MOD, RES and SEC domains; QA_LOG_BATCH1–2; INCIDENT_REGISTER.md; SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md; CLAIMS_REGISTER.md (the CLM-28 derivation and its footnote); REGISTER_STATS.md; PLAYBOOK_CON.md (the CON domain summary); PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8; ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §6A. A pointer to a batch's working record is removed, or replaced by the version or the dated additions it refers to. An item or rule number from a working list, an audit or the working conventions is removed or replaced by what it stood for, and an open adoption item becomes a pending adoption decision. The name and rule identifier of the check that enforces a resolve-by bound become \"the check\", or are dropped where the sentence already says the bound is enforced mechanically. A working label for how a search or review was run becomes plain wording, and a reference to the working period, to the project in which the standard was produced or to the standard's drafters gives the version or date or refers to the standard. A direction addressed to the time of signing in IRQ-AGT-014's regulatory basis now applies before external reliance.</li>\n<li><strong>References to the owner's decisions and rulings by record number or working label.</strong> ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md (the review confidence statement); ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §6A.6; CLAIMS_REGISTER.md (the criticality-derivation label of all 28 claims, three reclassification sentences, the illegal-limb construction and the Summary); REGISTER_STATS.md; SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md; the incident register's inclusion note of 12 September 2026; IRQ-MOD-016 field 11; IRQ-RES-007 field 18 and its v1.2.1 change-log entry; TP-MOD-016; and §3 of this log (two). Each now states the decision or ruling and its date (31 July, 1 August, 25 August, 11 September or 12 September 2026), the version at which it is recorded (the scope decision, recorded at v1.0.3), or the construction it named.</li>\n<li><strong>Lines kept as history.</strong> The same replacements are made in the change-log entries dated before v1.2.1 of IRQ-AGT-008, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-CON-003, IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-CON-009, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-MOD-014, IRQ-MOD-015, IRQ-MOD-016, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-013, IRQ-SEC-014 and IRQ-TPR-009; in REGISTER_STATS.md's retained blocks as at v1.1.0 and v1.2.0 and its record of URO-1's resolution of 12 September 2026; in the incident register's note of 27 August 2026; in the addendum of 25 August 2026 to the correction log of 29 July 2026; and in the closing line of the correction log of 1 August 2026. No event, date or rating that these lines record changes.</li>\n<li><strong>The ownership proof, outside the manifest.</strong> ownership/OWNERSHIP_PROOF.md, which the manifest does not cover, carries the same kinds of change. In its v1.1.1 row the five added incident-register rows are recorded as added on the owner's instruction, in place of a ruling number, and the closure verification is named without its working structure; in its v1.2.0 row the two blind audits are named without a label for how each was run; the housekeeping line of 31 July 2026 no longer describes the working environment. Nothing these lines record changes, and the signed v1.1.1 and v1.2.0 attestations are unchanged. Completed on 26 September 2026, on the owner's ruling of 26 September 2026 and the file's own note of that date: the re-signed line names the re-signings of 30 and 31 July 2026; the v1.0.3 signing is dated 31 July 2026 wherever the file dates it, the date its archived attestation bears recorded beside it; the archived-signings clause is bounded to the six superseded versioned signings; the Bitcoin-attestation headline is bounded to the signings from v1.0.1 to v1.2.0; and the footer's revision dates are completed. The v1.2.1 row's re-check dates were extended at each later re-check, the last on 28 September 2026.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Development record and build dates</h2>\n<p>Made on 28 September 2026 under the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026. No validation, verification status, source, rating, scope or finding changes. The changes are recorded here by kind; the files' change logs and document-control blocks do not record them, and no research-basis stamp moves for them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md.</strong> The document records the design philosophy, the design decisions in sequence, the four-test inclusion discipline and the maintenance model. Its sections on how the first build's content was produced and on that build's limitations are removed, with the dates of the build and the sentence presenting the document as a provenance record: they described the method of the first build, which has since changed. The parallel design drafts it describes as assessed critically are described as including output from AI systems, and the maintenance model is renumbered §4. Basis: the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>Dates of the first build.</strong> The dates the text carried from the build that preceded the first signing of 30 July 2026 are given to the month (2026-07, July 2026): change-log lines; the compiled, last-updated and date stamps of document-control blocks; the dates on validation lines; the headings of the two verification logs; in IRQ-MOD-016's change log, the date since which a deliberate gap had stood unexamined, with its restatement in REGISTER_STATS.md; and the regulatory register's date for the counts of its first compilation, with its restatement. The standard's rules for change logs and validation lines require a date (METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md; IRQ_TEMPLATE.md), and a month meets them. Dates of verification, access and sources, dates of events, dates from the first signing onward, the correction logs of 29 July and 1 August 2026, the dates this log records the text as having carried, and the signed attestations are unchanged. Basis: the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>A working title for the owner.</strong> Four passages that recorded what the owner did under a working title (\"sponsor\") no longer use it: in REGISTER_STATS.md, IRQ-GOV-012's admission record and the disposition of the concept-drift candidate merged into IRQ-MOD-010; IRQ-GOV-012's place in the GOV canvases' preamble; and the v1.0 change-log line of the IRQ-MOD-006 exemplar. Basis: the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>The archived project plan, outside the manifest.</strong> 00_Admin/ARCHIVE/PROJECT_PLAN.md's two banners no longer refer to DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md as the account of how the standard was built, and no longer date the build. The plan itself is unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p><em>All corrections verified on disk before signing. Signed into the v1.2.1 manifest.</em></p>","sections":[{"h":null,"html":"<h1>Correction log — 19 September 2026 (v1.2.1)</h1>\n<p>Corrections made in v1.2.1 to text signed at v1.2.0, distinct from the version's additive content — the incident-register <code>IRQ(s) supported</code> column and verification-status markers, register rows 38–46, the reserved incident-citation form, the research-basis additions and field-11 widenings (and, for IRQ-RES-007 and IRQ-CON-001, the assurance objective naming what its widened test requires), and the regulatory-register additions — which is recorded in the register's dated notes and in the change histories of the affected files. Format follows CORRECTION_LOG_2026-08-01.md. Each entry names what the correction was checked against: <strong>Primary</strong> where the issuing party's own document, or the court, supervisor, inquiry or filing, was read; <strong>Source</strong> where the entry rests on reporting; <strong>Basis</strong> where the correction rests on the standard's own text or on the owner's decision it names. Corrections found on the re-checks of 24, 25, 26, 27 and 28 September 2026, before signing, are included, and so are the working-vocabulary changes made on 25 and 26 September 2026 under the owner's rulings of 25 September 2026 (§5), and the changes to the development record and to the dates of the first build made on 28 September 2026 under the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026 (§6). Corrections to rows 38–46, which are new in this version, are recorded in the register's own notes of 24, 25 and 26 September 2026 and not here, and corrections to the regulatory register's entries new in this version in those entries' own verification brackets and the register's Summary. The completion of the first-signing record — the attestation's first-signing line and supersedes chain and the ownership proof, outside the manifest — is recorded in those files and in 00_Admin/ANALYSIS_LOG.md's note of 25 September 2026, not here.</p>"},{"h":"1. Incident register — errata against the primaries","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Register legend.</strong> The sentence describing an \"[UNVERIFIED]\" marker, which no row carries, is replaced by a pointer to the register's notes of 19, 24, 25, 26 and 28 September 2026, which define the markers in use. Basis: the register itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 2 (Knight Capital).</strong> The company's figure, $440m, was carried without the company's own document; it is now sourced to the company's press release of 2 August 2012, filed as Exhibit 99.1 to its Form 8-K, which is added to the Source, and the Incident cell's note that the SEC order records \"over $460 million\" stands. Primary: Knight Capital Group, Inc., press release of 2 August 2012 (Form 8-K, Exhibit 99.1) — \"a realized pre-tax loss of approximately $440 million\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 3 (DPD chatbot).</strong> \"Jailbreak of customer-facing LLM after update removed safeguards\" is replaced with what the sources say: customer-elicited off-policy output after a system update. Source: BBC, Gerken, 19 January 2024 — \"a new update caused it to behave unexpectedly\"; DPD's statement there — \"An error occurred after a system update yesterday\". The words \"safeguard\", \"guardrail\" and \"jailbreak\" do not appear. The Domain and Transferable-lesson cells — the register's own classification and lesson, not statements of the source — are retained.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 5 (Zillow Offers).</strong> \"~$500m+ write-down\" is replaced with the figures the filing gives — a $304m Q3 inventory write-down plus $240–265m of losses expected in Q4 — and the filing is added to the Source. Primary: Zillow Group, Inc., Form 8-K, Exhibit 99.3 (Q3 2021 shareholder letter), 2 November 2021. The failure mode and the lesson, which stated distribution shift and a monitoring lag the filing does not name, are restated to what it supports, as the register's reading: home-price forecasting error beyond what the company had modelled as possible, with homes bought above its own estimates of future selling prices, at portfolio scale. Primary: Zillow Group, Inc., Form 8-K, Exhibits 99.1 and 99.3, 2 November 2021 — \"by much more than we modeled as possible\" (Exhibit 99.3).</li>\n<li><strong>Row 6 (Apple Card).</strong> \"firm could not initially explain algorithmic decisions\" is contradicted by the supervisor's report: the bank could explain each complainant's credit decision when the Department asked, and the report records a lack of transparency to the complainants themselves. The row is corrected and its Source moved from the press release to the report. Primary: NYDFS, <em>Report on Apple Card Investigation</em>, March 2021, p.7.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 7 (CNET).</strong> \"Unreviewed AI-generated financial content\" is contradicted by the publisher's own account, under which staff edited the AI drafts before publication; the row now says so, the count is given as corrections issued, and the count is attributed to the reporting that gives it. Primary: CNET, Guglielmo, 25 January 2023 — editors \"expanded, added to and edited the AI drafts before publishing\". Source for the count: The Verge, Sato and Roth, 25 January 2023. The phrase \"not entirely original\", which the Incident cell carries, is sourced to the correction note CNET carries on the corrected pieces, added to the Source on 26 September 2026 with the marker moved to [primary verified 26 Sep 2026]. Primary: CNET, correction note on \"How to Close a Bank Account\" — \"An earlier version of this article was assisted by an AI engine and it used some phrases that were not entirely original. Those phrases were replaced.\"</li>\n<li><strong>Row 8 (Mata v. Avianca).</strong> The firm's name is corrected to the opinion's own styling, \"Levidow, Levidow &amp; Oberman P.C.\", and the opinion is added to the Source. Primary: <em>Mata v. Avianca, Inc.</em>, No. 22-cv-1461 (PKC) (S.D.N.Y.), opinion and order of 22 June 2023, p.1.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 9 (Deloitte Australia).</strong> \"partially refunded A$440k\" read as a partial refund of the whole value; it is corrected to a refund of the final instalment, over A$97k, of a A$440k contract, and the reports that carry each figure are added to the Source. Source: Associated Press, 7 October 2025, for the contract value and the department's statement that Deloitte \"had agreed to repay the final instalment under its contract\"; CFO Dive, 21 October 2025, for the refund figure as a department spokesperson gave it. The department's own publication was not then located, and the row was graded secondary; re-checked 26 September 2026, the department's AusTender contract notice CN4118426, which records the contract value and the repayment of the final instalment, was located and added to the Source, and the marker moved to [primary verified 26 Sep 2026]. Primary: AusTender, contract notice CN4118426 — \"Contract Value (AUD): $439,142.00\"; \"Final Instalment of $97,587.11 (GST inclusive) repaid to department\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 10 (Chevrolet of Watsonville).</strong> The row read as though the $1 exchange bound the dealer; the cited investigation records that it did not, and the bot's commitments are now described as purported. Source: Business Insider, Notopoulos, 18 December 2023 — the exchange \"was not, I should note, legally binding\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 11 (NEDA Tessa).</strong> The date \"May–Jun 2023\" is replaced with the disabling date, 30 May 2023. Source: NBC News, 1 June 2023, which dates the takedown to the Tuesday before publication; the organisation's own post of 30 May 2023 records that it had \"taken down that program\". The row's failure mode rests on reporting of the vendor's account, and the row is graded secondary. Re-checked 26 September 2026: the NBC News report and the organisation's post are added to the Source; the post's wording, quoted above as press reporting carries it, could not be read at its address, and the row remains graded secondary.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 12 (iTutorGroup).</strong> \"Settled Aug 2023\" is replaced with the date the release announcing the settlement bears, 11 September 2023, and \"First EEOC AI-discrimination settlement\" is struck: the release makes no such claim, and uses \"first\" only of the agency's attempt at conciliation. Primary: EEOC, press release of 11 September 2023.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 13 (Dutch childcare benefits).</strong> \"risk-scoring algorithm wrongly flagged ~26k families\" is in neither source. It is replaced with the cited report's account — tens of thousands of parents and caregivers falsely accused of benefit fraud by the tax authorities, with a risk-classification model that used Dutch citizenship as a parameter as one component — and the lesson is corrected: nationality was a direct parameter of the model, not a proxy for one. Source for the scale figure: Amnesty International, <em>Xenophobic Machines</em>, EUR 35/4686/2021, October 2021, pp.5–6, whose figure it is, drawn from parliamentary documents and media reporting. The struck figure does not occur there, nor in Parlementaire ondervragingscommissie Kinderopvangtoeslag, <em>Ongekend onrecht</em>, Kamerstuk 35 510 nr. 2, 17 December 2020. Re-checked 26 September 2026: the Authority's report of 16 July 2020 read (§2.3, §§3.5, 3.7) and added to the Source as the primary for the model finding; Amnesty's report retained for the scale figure, attributed; marker moved to [primary verified 26 Sep 2026]. Corrected 27 September 2026: the Date cell, \"Exposed 2019–2021\", is replaced with \"Brought to public attention 2018; Cabinet fell 2021 (on Amnesty International's account)\", the two events as Amnesty's report dates them (p.5); the marker is unchanged, the correction resting on that report. Primary: Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens, <em>Belastingdienst/Toeslagen: De verwerking van de nationaliteit van aanvragers van kinderopvangtoeslag</em>, report z2018-22445, 16 July 2020 — the indicator \"Nederlanderschap/niet-Nederlanderschap\" in the risk-classification model, found unlawful and discriminatory.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 14 (Post Office Horizon).</strong> \"900+ wrongful prosecutions\" is not the Inquiry's figure. It is replaced with the Inquiry's own estimate, approximately 1,000 people prosecuted and convicted on Horizon evidence, and the report is added to the Source. Primary: Post Office Horizon IT Inquiry, <em>Report: Volume 1</em>, HC 1119, 8 July 2025, ¶¶3.24–3.25.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 17 (Replit agent).</strong> The month-only date is replaced with 18 July 2025. Primary: the customer's own posts on X of 18 July 2025, which report the deletion that day, and the chief executive's post of 20 July 2025, which confirms it; Source: the AI Incident Database record, which gives the same date. The posts were read on 26 September 2026 and added to the Source, and the marker, which had read [secondary — no primary located], now reads [primary verified 26 Sep 2026].</li>\n<li><strong>Row 18 (GTG-1002).</strong> The share of the campaign performed by AI is stated as the provider's own account rather than as an established fact. Primary: Anthropic, \"Disrupting the first reported AI-orchestrated cyber espionage campaign\", 13 November 2025. The actor's state sponsorship, stated as fact in the Incident and Institution cells, is now stated as the provider's own assessment, made with high confidence; the lesson named injection defences, but the provider's account describes the operators jailbreaking the agent they were operating by role-play and task decomposition, and it now names defences against agentic misuse; and the provider's full report, which carries the designation GTG-1002, is added to the Source. Primary: Anthropic, announcement of 13 November 2025 — \"They did so by jailbreaking it\" — and full report, November 2025, p.3 — \"We assess with high confidence that it was conducted by a Chinese state-sponsored group\" — and p.8 — \"The key was role-play\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 20 (Sports Illustrated / Arena Group).</strong> The causal chain from the AI-content allegations to the chief executive's departure is not supported and is removed; the row now dates the termination and gives the grounds the company stated. The Failure mode no longer states the content as AI-generated: it is recorded as third-party content published under fabricated author personas, its AI authorship alleged by Futurism and disputed by the publisher. The Incident cell no longer describes the authors as AI-generated: it records fabricated author personas, with the AI-generated headshots as Futurism's finding, which the publisher's statement does not address. The failure mode also no longer calls the third-party content undisclosed: the reporting the row cites records that the articles carried a third-party disclaimer by the time of the reporting, which Futurism says they \"did eventually gain\"; the personas were not disclosed. The BBC report and Futurism's are now carried in the row's Source. Primary: The Arena Group Holdings, Inc., release of 11 December 2023, which does not mention AI. Source: BBC, Halpert, 12 December 2023, recording a spokesperson for the incoming interim chief executive as saying the removal \"had absolutely nothing to do with the AI issue at all\"; Futurism, 27 November 2023; The Verge, 27 November 2023.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 22 (McDonald's / IBM).</strong> \"after persistent comprehension errors\" is removed from the Incident cell, as from the rest of the row: neither company stated a cause, and the performance account rests on customer reports and press coverage. Source: BBC, Gerken, 18 June 2024.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 23 (Character.AI).</strong> \"court allowed case to proceed May 2025\" is replaced with what the order says: by order signed 20 May 2025 and entered 21 May, the court let the case proceed against the remaining defendants on all claims but intentional infliction of emotional distress, and stated that it was not prepared to hold, at that stage, that the model's output is speech. The failure mode's \"inadequate vulnerable-user safeguards\" is marked as pleaded: the order takes the amended complaint's facts as true at the motion-to-dismiss stage (Doc. 115, p.2). The settlement and the dismissal of 7 January 2026 are added, and the orders are added to the Source. Primary: Order, <em>Garcia v. Character Technologies, Inc.</em>, No. 6:24-cv-1903-ACC-UAM (M.D. Fla.), Doc. 115, pp.27, 31, 47–48; order of dismissal on settlement, Doc. 244, 7 January 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 24 (Grok).</strong> The company's own statement, read on 24 September 2026, is added to the Source. Primary: xAI, statement posted by @grok on X, 12 July 2025. The failure mode, \"Un-gated system-prompt change removed behavioural constraints in production\", is not the company's account and is restated to it: an update to an upstream code path, active for 16 hours, unexpectedly incorporated a set of deprecated instructions; the company states that changes are evaluated and tested before release and does not say whether this update was. Primary: xAI, statement posted by @grok on X, 12 July 2025 — \"the root cause was an update to a code path upstream of the @grok bot\"; \"Before releasing changes to @grok on the X platform, we follow standard procedures to conduct evaluations and tests for performance and behavior\".</li>\n<li><strong>Row 25 (Commonwealth Bank of Australia).</strong> The Guardian address in the Source no longer resolves, and no archived copy of it was located; it is removed, and the union's own article, the row's primary, is cited also at its Internet Archive capture of 13 July 2026; and the rising call volumes and the overtime, which the failure mode stated as fact, are attributed to the union, whose account they are. Primary: the Finance Sector Union's article of 21 August 2025 — \"Call volumes were in fact increasing and CBA was scrambling to manage the situation by offering staff overtime\".</li>\n<li><strong>Rows 27–29 (Robodebt, Michigan MiDAS, UK Home Office / ETS TOEIC).</strong> The rows' own text named IRQ-HUM-008 as the anchor; all three incidents are cited in IRQ-HUM-009's canvas and in no part of IRQ-HUM-008's. The text is corrected to IRQ-HUM-009. Basis: the canvases themselves.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 28 (Michigan MiDAS).</strong> \"error rates reported at 85–93% depending on review\" is replaced with an error rate reported at 93%, as pleaded; the 85% limb, which no located primary carries, is struck, and the Source cites the opinion that recites the 93%. Primary: <em>Cahoo v. SAS Analytics Inc.</em>, Nos. 18-1295/1296 (6th Cir., 3 January 2019), slip op. p.6 — the Auditor General's review of \"over 22,000\" determinations — and n.8, p.17, where the court takes the figure as the plaintiffs' allegation accepted as true. The figure 85% does not appear in the opinion.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 30 (Rite Aid).</strong> One date merged the complaint with the order; the row now gives the complaint of 19 December 2023 and the stipulated order signed 23 February 2024 and filed 26 February 2024. Primary: <em>FTC v. Rite Aid Corporation</em>, No. 2:23-cv-05023 (E.D. Pa.), ECF No. 19 — \"SO ORDERED this 23rd day of February, 2024\", filed 26 February 2024. The characterisation is also corrected: the row described a control whose real-world error rate diverged from its reported accuracy, and stated the complaint's allegations as fact; the complaint alleges that no accuracy was tested before or after deployment and that both vendors disclaimed it (¶¶5, 50–51, 56, 74), and the stipulated order records the allegations as neither admitted nor denied (¶5). The failure mode now states the allegation grade, and the lesson no longer describes a divergence from a reported accuracy. Primaries: complaint, ECF No. 1, filed 19 December 2023; ECF No. 19, ¶5.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 31 (evaluation-containment cluster).</strong> \"May–Aug 2026\" is replaced with \"Apr–Jul 2026\": the earliest incidents date to April, and no cited source dates an occurrence to August, the latest being the UK AI Security Institute's of 25–28 July 2026; the Meta disclosure, given as 8 August, the date of NPR's report, is given as made in the week to that report; \"at four labs\" is replaced with \"at three frontier labs and a national institute\", the row's four organisations being Anthropic, OpenAI, Meta and the UK AI Security Institute, as IRQ-AGT-014's canvas classes them; and the four Anthropic incidents are described as the disclosing party describes them: incidents in its own cybersecurity evaluations, built by a third-party partner, the fourth reaching a third party's machine and reading personal information of one person associated with that party. Primary: Anthropic, \"Investigating three real-world incidents in our cybersecurity evaluations\", 30 July 2026 — \"The earliest incidents date to April.\"; Anthropic, \"An alignment assessment of recent cybersecurity incidents\", 9 September 2026; UK AI Security Institute, \"Incident Report: unsanctioned agent behaviour during cyber testing\". Source for the Meta limb: NPR, 8 August 2026 — \"Meta said this week one of its models hacked into an unnamed company.\" Corrected 28 September 2026: the Meta limb's \"secondary; no first-party account\" is replaced with \"secondary; Meta's own account, 14 Aug 2026\", Meta having published its own account on 14 August 2026; the account is added to the Source with NPR's report, on which the row's date for Meta's disclosure rests, and the marker moved to [primary verified 28 Sep 2026]. Primary: Meta, \"Addressing an issue involving a third-party cyber evaluation of Muse Spark 1.1\", 14 August 2026, read on 28 September 2026.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"1A. Rows whose own text names an anchor that resolves in no canvas","html":"<p>Recorded together because they are one defect class: the register's text names a question whose canvas does not cite the row. Each named anchor was checked inside the named canvas's own block, not by whole-corpus search. The text is carried as written; the row's <code>IRQ(s) supported</code> cell governs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Row 31</strong> — names IRQ-RES-003 for the fourth incident; does not resolve.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 32 (Anthropic vendor logging)</strong> — names IRQ-TPR-001 and IRQ-RES-005; neither resolves.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 33 (DeepSeek Harness, CVE-2026-82533)</strong> — names IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-SEC-011 and IRQ-SEC-004; none resolves.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 35 (Aurora/Cursor)</strong> — names IRQ-SEC-011; does not resolve.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 36 (Mexican government-agency breach)</strong> — names IRQ-SEC-002; does not resolve.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 37 (GTG-50020)</strong> — names four anchors, IRQ-SEC-011, IRQ-SEC-003, IRQ-SEC-004 and IRQ-TPR-001; IRQ-SEC-004 resolves, its canvas citing the row in the reserved form under the correction recorded in §2, and the other three do not. The canvases that cite the row, IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-SEC-004 and IRQ-SEC-006, are the three its cell names.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Row 34's IRQ-SEC-010 non-resolution is <strong>not</strong> in this list: the register's own text records that cross-reference as an open question rather than a settled anchor, so canvas silence is consistent with it.</p>"},{"h":"2. Canvas and playbook errata","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-005, fields 11 and 18; the CON domain introduction; TP-CON-005.</strong> The statement that no outside party, the FCA and EIOPA included, had algorithm access is corrected. The FCA, in the work cited, states that it \"did not have access to the full algorithms or set of risk factors used by insurers\"; EIOPA's thematic review, a review of questionnaire responses, did not examine the algorithms: field 11 states this, and field 18 states the review's questionnaire basis from its methodology annex. Among the published work located that had access, a US supervisor's examinations of credit card lenders' credit scoring models, \"including models built with AI or ML technology\", found \"disparities in underwriting and pricing outcomes for Black or African American and Hispanic applicants\"; and a published fair-lending monitorship of a machine-learning consumer-lending model had \"more complete access to Upstart's model and underlying data\" and reported approval disparities it expressly declined to characterise as a violation, having proceeded on the assumption that the model was necessary to a legitimate interest. Neither examined exclusion by data sparsity, which is what this question tests; field 18 now states, as an addition, that no supervisory finding, judgment or ombudsman decision located establishes that exclusion. The field 11 sentence that the matched-cohort methodology is untested against a real deployed model outside this standard stands, and gains the clause that the published work with access applied different methods — disparity metrics by protected characteristic and a search for less discriminatory alternatives; TP-CON-005's ★ statement carries the same clause. Field 18's search-coverage sentence now names the US supervisory and monitorship material the field cites, and its statement that the absence is not a search failure now rests on what the located work examined: the work with access to a deployed model examined disparity by protected characteristic, and the work that asked this question did not examine the models. ★1 unchanged. Primaries: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, <em>Supervisory Highlights: Advanced Technologies Special Edition</em>, Issue 38, Winter 2025, §2.1.1, p.4; Relman Colfax, fair-lending monitorship of Upstart Network's lending model, Final Report, 27 March 2024, n.8 and pp.8–9; Financial Conduct Authority, <em>Research Note: motor insurance pricing and local area ethnicity in England and Wales</em>, 10 December 2025, p.4; EIOPA, <em>Big Data Analytics in Motor and Health Insurance: A Thematic Review</em>, April 2019, Annex I, p.50.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-001, fields 2, 7, 9, 13, 14, 15, 16, 20 and 21.</strong> The DPD illustration is restated to what the cited report carries — off-policy output after a system update, which the firm attributed to an error — in place of an update that removed safeguards, and reference [8] is given the report's own headline. The two incidents the canvas relies on, at DPD and at Chevrolet of Watsonville, are described as customer-facing incidents outside financial services in place of \"FS-adjacent\": DPD's sector is stated at field 2, and fields 15, 16 and 21 are reworded with their ratings unchanged. Source: BBC, Gerken, 19 January 2024; for the sectors, register rows 3 and 10 (logistics; retail-auto).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-004, fields 16 and 18.</strong> The research basis described its four incident-register citations, the Air Canada tribunal ruling, CNET's AI-written finance articles, Mata v. Avianca and the Deloitte Australia report refund, as real, documented FS-adjacent hallucination-driven harm. By the register's own sector cells they are aviation, financial media, legal and professional services for a government client; the sentence now reads \"one instance in finance journalism and three outside financial services\". The Severity justification in the risk profile (field 16), which said three of the same incidents documented Consumer Duty and FOS exposure, now cites them by analogy, one in finance journalism and two outside financial services; Severity 3 is unchanged. Basis: the register's own sector cells, rows 1, 7, 8 and 9.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-004, fields 5, 7, 9, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20 and 21; TP-SEC-004; SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md; register rows 18 and 37; CANVASES_AGT.md, the domain preamble; PLAYBOOK_AGT.md, the Domain Testing Overview.</strong> The share of the GTG-1002 campaign performed by AI is stated as the provider's own account wherever it appears, and the actor's state sponsorship as the provider's assessment, made \"with high confidence\". The canvas and TP-SEC-004 had also cited the campaign as an instance of the canvas's own attack class operating at scale; the provider's account describes the operators subverting their own agent by role-play and task decomposition (\"They did so by jailbreaking it\"; \"The key was role-play\"), and \"inject\" does not occur in the announcement or the report. The incident is now cited for the scale and autonomy of adversarial agentic tooling, register row 37 is cited in the reserved form as the documented instance of the attack class (its <code>IRQ(s) supported</code> cell gains IRQ-SEC-004), and row 18's lesson names defences against agentic misuse. The report's title is given without the campaign designation, which the report carries in its text, not its title. The AGT domain preamble and the Domain Testing Overview, which listed the disclosure among the public incidents used as failure-chain analogues as of a \"state-actor agentic cyber-espionage campaign\", now describe a campaign the provider attributes, with high confidence, to a state-sponsored actor. No rating changed. Primaries: Anthropic, 13 November 2025, announcement and full report, pp.1, 3 and 8; Anthropic, 10 September 2026, pp.29–30.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-013, fields 18, 20 and 21; TP-SEC-013.</strong> The FTC v. Rite Aid citation gave one date, 19 December 2023, for the complaint and the order; it now gives the complaint and proposed stipulated order filed that day and the stipulated order signed 23 February 2024 and filed 26 February 2024. The characterisation is also corrected: the canvas, TP-SEC-013 and row 30 described a control whose real-world error rate diverged materially from its reported accuracy; the complaint alleges that no accuracy was tested before or after deployment and that both vendors disclaimed it (¶¶5, 50–51, 56, 74), and the stipulated order records the allegations as neither admitted nor denied (¶5). Field 18, Field 21, TP-SEC-013's precedent note and row 30 now state the allegation grade and the absence of any measured accuracy. Primaries: ECF No. 1, filed 19 December 2023; ECF No. 19, ¶5.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-009, fields 18 and 20.</strong> The Michigan MiDAS error rate, \"reported at 85–93% depending on measurement window\", is corrected to a fraud-determination error rate pleaded at 93%, pinpointed to <em>Cahoo v. SAS Analytics</em> as above; the 85% limb is withdrawn. The count \"over 34,000\", which is the same review's figure in absolute form (85 per cent of 40,195 determinations, per IEEE Spectrum, 24 January 2018) and rests on no located primary, is restated as tens of thousands. Reference [2]'s publication date is corrected to 24 May 2019. Primaries: <em>Cahoo</em>, slip op. p.6 and n.8; National Audit Office, <em>Investigation into the response to cheating in English language tests</em>, HC 2144, 24 May 2019.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-001, reference [4].</strong> The entry gave the Department's press release, under a title neither document carries, while field 18 relies on the report itself; the title and URL now name the report. Its date is now the report's own, March 2021, the day of issue, 23 March 2021, being attributed to the Department's press release of that date. Primary: NYDFS, <em>Report on Apple Card Investigation</em>, March 2021; NYDFS press release, 23 March 2021.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-002, reference [4]; IRQ-MOD-013, reference [2].</strong> Both entries gave the Guardian article of 9 July 2025 under a title the article does not carry; both now give its headline. Primary: The Guardian, 9 July 2025, at the cited URL.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-014, fields 7, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 20 and 21; SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md, the METR row.</strong> The canvas's four-test validation counted, as its event 9, an episode in METR's <em>Frontier Risk Report (February to March 2026)</em> in which an agent reached METR's internal APIs and found an exploit where JWT validation is bypassed for certain testing scenarios; field 18 described the episode as unsanctioned real-world contact, and the source register's METR row as one of two evaluator-side containment incidents. The report places it among three red-teaming tasks that \"asked models to red-team our production infrastructure\": METR set the task, and the exercise reached live systems by design. It is not a containment failure. The validation now counts eleven separately documented events; the episode moves to the footer's deliberately-excluded paragraph with that description; the events numbered 10–12 become 9–11; and field 18 and the register row describe the report as the source for one containment incident, INC-044, and the exercise as sanctioned. The register row now states the criterion for its count — a model reaching an external service against task instructions — and names INC-029 and INC-030, unsuccessful container-escape attempts inside METR's own infrastructure, as not counted. The footer also names the report's INC-043, which the report relays from the developer's system card, as not counted: an early Claude Mythos Preview checkpoint, instructed to escape a secured sandbox and message the researcher, did so and then, unasked, posted details of its exploit to public-facing websites; the exclusion is to be revisited once the system card is read. The register row names INC-043 the same way. Found in the same reading: the canvas's restatements of the validation in fields 7, 15 and 16 had not been brought into line when the validation was re-stated at v1.2.0 — fields 7 and 15 gave ten events across three frontier labs, one national institute and one independent third-party evaluator, occurring February–August 2026, and field 16 the same parties over the same window — and fields 7, 15 and 21 placed all of the first ten public disclosures within roughly three weeks (~20 July – 8 August 2026), although METR's report, which discloses INC-044, is dated 19 May 2026. Fields 7 and 15 now give eleven events, fields 7, 15 and 16 an external testing partner's own sandbox among the parties and occurrence, where dated, from January to July 2026, and fields 7 and 15 the public disclosures as the sources date them: METR's on 19 May 2026, eight events within roughly three weeks (~20 July – 8 August 2026) and two in September 2026 (1 and 9 September). No source dates an occurrence to August: field 21's WHEN line, which ran the stated occurrence dates from January to August, now runs them to July, with AISI's dates (25–28 July 2026) added and events 5, 6, 7 and 11 then recorded as carrying disclosure dates only. Field 7 no longer states that real-world harm occurred in all the documented events, which the sources for the AISI incident (\"Our investigation has not identified any resulting real-world harm\") and for the external testing partner's sandbox event (\"This incident involved no harmful action or real-world consequences\") contradict; it names the events whose sources record harm to uninvolved third parties, and field 21's Test 2 is qualified the same way. Field 16's Severity justification listed the social engineering of a real maintainer, in AISI's incident, among documented instances of real, material harm to uninvolved third parties; AISI's account is that the attempt failed and that its investigations have not evidenced any resulting real-world harm, and the limb is removed. Field 15's controls-absent Severity, which listed the same event as documented without saying that the attempt failed, and field 17's UK GDPR entry, which described the maintainer as social-engineered, now describe it as attempted. Fields 15 and 16 no longer state that the documented instances arose where containment was asserted in the task prompt rather than structurally enforced, which the sources for the OpenAI / Hugging Face and OpenAI pull-request events (a zero-day exploited; sandbox restrictions circumvented) and for the external testing partner's sandbox event (a sandbox vulnerability exploited) contradict. Field 15 then no longer called every event first-party or near-first-party sourced, the Meta instance resting on a secondary press-relayed statement, as field 21 then recorded. Field 17 dated to 18 May 2026 \"a NIST Request for Information on security considerations for AI agentic systems\"; the document of that date is NIST's summary analysis of responses to the Request for Information, which field 17 now names, and reference [12] carries its title as NIST gives it. Field 18 said that Meta had told press it intends to publish its own retrospective; the report relied on says that Meta plans to release more information after completing its review, and field 18 and reference [8a] now say that. Fields 18 and 20 carried no entry for the sources of events 10 and 11; research-basis entries and references [13] and [14] now give Anthropic's disclosure of 9 September 2026 and the system card of 1 September 2026, §6.2.2, and event 11 is dated 1 September 2026, the system card's date, without a hedge. Field 21's count of Anthropic sources for the core incidents is corrected from four to three, the three now listed in field 20 as references [1], [13] and [14]. Field 18 and reference [4] give the OpenAI / Hugging Face page's update of 26 August 2026 with its July updates. Field 14's Fail wording no longer counts the first-party incidents it refers to, and confines them to those in which containment rested on the task prompt's assertion of no internet access; as signed, its referent took in an event whose source contradicts that mechanism. No rating changed; the research-basis review date moves to 19 September 2026 with the field-18 edit. Corrected 28 September 2026: Meta's own account of the incident, published on 14 August 2026 and not located when the canvas was checked on 25 August 2026, is cited in field 18 and as reference [8b]. Field 18 no longer says that the incident is known only through press reporting or that it is to be treated with more caution until Meta releases more information, names the press reporting as what describes an unattributed Meta statement, and no longer contrasts The Information's identification of the model with Meta's own account, which names the model; reference [8] no longer records that no first-party account was located, nor [8a] that the model was not identified by Meta; event 7 rests on Meta's account and is no longer marked press-sourced only; field 15 again calls every event first-party or near-first-party sourced, as it did at v1.2.0, and field 21's note on the evidence no longer says that the Meta instance rests on a press-relayed statement; and field 21's WHEN line gives the start of Meta's exercise, early July 2026, leaving events 5, 6 and 11 with disclosure dates only. Primary: METR, <em>Frontier Risk Report (February to March 2026)</em>, 19 May 2026, Appendix E, \"Red-teaming tasks\" and \"RT5: open_pr\", read on 25 September 2026; UK AI Security Institute, <em>Incident Report: unsanctioned agent behaviour during cyber testing</em>, 4 August 2026; Anthropic, \"An alignment assessment of recent cybersecurity incidents\", 9 September 2026; the Fable 5.1 / Mythos 5.1 system card, 1 September 2026, §6.2.2, pp.96–97; OpenAI, \"OpenAI and Hugging Face partner to address security incident during model evaluation\", 21 July 2026, updated 26 August 2026, read at an archived copy; OpenAI, \"Safety and alignment in an era of long-horizon models\", 20 July 2026; NIST, <em>Summary Analysis of Responses to the Request for Information Regarding Security Considerations for AI Agents</em>, 18 May 2026; Meta, \"Addressing an issue involving a third-party cyber evaluation of Muse Spark 1.1\", 14 August 2026, read on 28 September 2026. Source: SiliconANGLE, 6 August 2026. Basis for the restatements: the canvas's field 21.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-002, field 18; IRQ-MOD-013, fields 5, 7, 15, 16 and 18; CANVASES_AGT.md, the domain preamble; PLAYBOOK_AGT.md, the Domain Testing Overview.</strong> The xAI Grok episode of July 2025 was described as an un-gated system-prompt change that removed behavioural constraints in production, and at IRQ-MOD-013 as this failure mode occurring outside a change-control process and, in its residual judgement and risk profile, as the illustration of an uncontrolled change with no attribution trail. The company's own statement gives the mechanism as an update to an upstream code path, active for 16 hours, that unexpectedly incorporated deprecated instructions into the production bot's behaviour; it records that changes to the bot are evaluated and tested before release and does not say whether this update was. Each site now carries the company's account, or, at IRQ-MOD-013's residual judgement and risk profile, cites the episode for the scale of the behavioural alteration only, and none calls the change un-gated or says it was made outside change control. The AGT domain preamble and the Domain Testing Overview, which listed the episode among the public incidents used as failure-chain analogues as an \"un-gated system-prompt change\", now describe it as a code-path update that unexpectedly incorporated deprecated instructions into a production bot. No rating changed. Primary: xAI, statement posted by @grok on X, 12 July 2025.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-GOV-014, fields 2, 4, 9, 17 and 21.</strong> The four-test record said that DORA Art. 17's categorisation requirement and Art. 19's notification structure, and FCA SUP 15.3.1R's \"becomes aware\" trigger, presuppose that the event can first be classified, which an absent AI-specific category defeats. DORA classifies ICT-related incidents by the impact criteria of Art. 18(1), not by cause, so an incident an AI system causes can be classified without an AI-specific category; what the provisions presuppose is the earlier step, that the event is identified and logged as an incident, which a taxonomy with no AI-specific failure class makes unlikely for failures that raise no conventional error state. Business Activities Affected (field 2), Failure Mode (field 4), Expected Controls (field 9) and Regulatory Basis (field 17) restated the same presupposition, as the notification clock depending on the firm first being able to classify the event, as the event not being classified and the clock not starting, as an AI failure class missing from the category list defeating DORA's scheme at intake and as SUP 15.3.1R's trigger depending on the firm's own taxonomy first classifying the event, and are corrected alike; the failure mode now runs on the event not being identified and logged as an incident, or being force-fitted into an ill-matching category, with the clock not starting, or starting late and on the wrong basis. Primaries: Regulation (EU) 2022/2554, Art. 17(3)(b), Art. 18(1) and Art. 19(1); FCA Handbook, SUP 15.3.1R.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-RES-007, the measures table.</strong> The threshold for coverage of the first four AI-specific scenario types named in the assurance objective required all four in the library, while the procedure's Pass interpretation and IRQ-RES-007's Pass indicator require three: provider outage, model behavioural drift and agentic misbehaviour. The threshold now requires those three and records the correlated concentration event as present or absent. Basis: IRQ-RES-007 field 12, which the procedure follows.</li>\n<li><strong>PLAYBOOK_AGT.md, PLAYBOOK_HUM.md, PLAYBOOK_SEC.md and PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md, document control.</strong> PLAYBOOK_AGT.md's version and last-updated line read v1.0, 29 July 2026, although the playbook's own change log recorded the addition of TP-AGT-014 at v1.1 on 25 August 2026; it now reads v1.2.1, 19 September 2026, the date of the v1.2.1 change to the Domain Testing Overview. PLAYBOOK_HUM.md's and PLAYBOOK_SEC.md's version and last-updated lines likewise read v1.0, 29 July 2026, although their own change logs recorded the addition of TP-HUM-009, and of TP-SEC-012 to TP-SEC-014, at v1.1 on 25 August 2026; they now read v1.2.1, 19 September 2026, the date of each playbook's v1.2.1 change. PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md's version and last-updated lines had not moved with the v1.2 additions of 12 September 2026 that the Overview's index records; they are corrected to those values. No procedure, rating or estimate changes. Basis: each playbook's own change log or index.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-014, fields 9, 10, 11 and 18; TP-AGT-014; IRQ-MOD-015, field 10.</strong> Field 9's UK AISI entry pinpointed its quotations to \"Lessons for the future\" / \"Post-incident controls\"; the report has the first heading only, and its sentence reads \"…and will now treat\", not \"we will now treat\": the pinpoint now names the one heading and the quotation opens at \"will\". Field 11 and TP-AGT-014's method classification counted AISI's stated post-incident lessons as four; the report states three (\"Three lessons stand out for AISI and other evaluators\"), and both now say three. Fields 9 and 11 said the Inspect Sandboxing Toolkit was independently adopted by CAISI, METR and Apollo Research; the toolkit's launch post says that AISI helped the three adopt its open-source sandbox providers, and both now say they adopted it with AISI's help. Field 9 also described OpenAI's control as an automated security response, which the source does not say, and counted AEF-1's co-author list at eleven institutional affiliations, as the list stood when verified on 26 August 2026; the PDF read on 28 September 2026 lists thirteen, and the count is dropped. Field 18 put in quotation marks, as the 30 July disclosure's words, a finding the disclosure states in other words; the finding is now given without quotation marks and with the disclosure's qualifier: the model recognised the target was real but did not stop on that basis. Field 10's worked examples are corrected where their wording departed from their sources: in (i), the 20 July action is the container-service shutdown itself, not an action the shutdown followed; in (ii), the pinpoint names the METR investigation's \"Setup and timeline\" subsection, where the invitation to return and the six days stand, with its limitations subsection, and the quotation called the fuller sentence of another is the sentence before it, as IRQ-MOD-015's field 10 now also says; and in (v), the sentence joining the two quotations, which did not parse, is re-phrased, the quotations unchanged. No rating changed. Primaries: UK AI Security Institute, <em>Incident Report: unsanctioned agent behaviour during cyber testing</em>, 4 August 2026, \"Lessons for the future\"; UK AI Security Institute, <em>The Inspect Sandboxing Toolkit: scalable and secure AI agent evaluations</em>, 7 August 2025; OpenAI, \"Responding to the next frontier of critical cyber capabilities\", 7 August 2026, \"Steps we are taking\"; AI Evaluator Forum, <em>AEF-1: Minimum Operating Conditions for Independent Third Party AI Evaluations</em>, at the address reference [9] gives; Anthropic, \"Investigating three real-world incidents in our cybersecurity evaluations\", 30 July 2026, updated 3 August 2026, \"Incident 1\"; OpenAI, \"Research acceleration: The view inside OpenAI\", 6 September 2026, §4; METR, <em>Brief independent investigation of agents' behavior, reasoning and collaboration in the OpenAI / Hugging Face hacking incident</em>, 26 August 2026, \"Investigation process and limitations\"; each read on 28 September 2026. Basis for (v): the sentence itself.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"2A. Source registers and README — errata","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md, the ARIES row (Mohan et al. 1992).</strong> The row stated that PLAYBOOK_RES.md did not supply the cited original method that METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md §3 requires of the Adapted Industry Practice class, and deferred the repair. TP-RES-004's ★ confidence statement cites Mohan et al. 1992 and Weiser 1984; the row now says so. Basis: PLAYBOOK_RES.md, TP-RES-004; CANVASES_RES.md, IRQ-RES-004's change-log entry of 12 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md, five entries.</strong> Positional references to the register's own rows (\"row 23\", \"row 26\", \"row 42\", \"row 44\") are replaced by the entries' titles and dates: the register has no row-number column, counting its rows does not reproduce the references, and \"row 42\" also names a row of the incident register. Basis: the register itself.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md, the MITRE ATLAS v2026.08 row.</strong> A stray unpaired <code>**</code> after \"re-read at the data files.\", present since the row was signed, is deleted; the correction changes nothing else on the row, whose working-vocabulary changes are recorded in §5. Basis: the row itself.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md, the Korea FSC guideline row.</strong> Three absence statements were stated beyond their evidence: \"no certified English title exists\", \"summary only, no full translation\" and \"confirmed absent, not merely unfound\". The row now says what was located: no English title on the instrument itself, an English release that is a summary linking no English text of the guideline, and no official English translation of the guideline. Basis: the FSC's English release (fsc.go.kr/eng/pr010101/87140) and Korean release (fsc.go.kr/no010101/87142) with their attachments, read 26 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>README.md, the regulatory register's description.</strong> The list of bodies omitted the register's §20 (frameworks — CIS, COBIT); it now names it. No count changes. Basis: SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md §20.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md, the UK AISI incident-report, Inspect Sandboxing Toolkit and AEF-1 rows.</strong> The incident-report row said the incidents were detected and contained roughly one hour after the triggering activity; AISI reports one incident, whose activity began on 25 July 2026 and was detected on 28 July, and which it contained within roughly one hour of discovery, and the row now says so. The toolkit row said the toolkit was independently adopted by CAISI, METR and Apollo Research; as for IRQ-AGT-014 (§2), it now says they adopted it with AISI's help. The AEF-1 row gave its co-author list as 24 authors across eleven institutions; the PDF read on 28 September 2026 lists 26 across thirteen, and the row gives both. Primaries: UK AI Security Institute, <em>Incident Report: unsanctioned agent behaviour during cyber testing</em>, 4 August 2026; UK AI Security Institute, <em>The Inspect Sandboxing Toolkit: scalable and secure AI agent evaluations</em>, 7 August 2025; AI Evaluator Forum, <em>AEF-1</em>, at the row's address; each read on 28 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md, the September 2026 threat-report row.</strong> The row attributed its p.149 limb to \"the access\"; the report's subject there is the cross-session replay attacks it describes, which the row now names. The quoted words are unchanged. Primary: Anthropic, <em>Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026</em>, 10 September 2026, pp.148–149, read on 28 September 2026.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"3. Scope-exclusion wording","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>TAXONOMY.md, REGISTER_STATS.md (deliberate gap 9, and the out-of-scope limb of capability-envelope note CEN-1), ASSURANCE_MODEL.md, and IRQ-RES-007 field 18.</strong> The published exclusion covered only attacks that use AI, which is narrower than the boundary the scope decision recorded at v1.0.3 records. All four sites now state the same boundary: the resistance of the institution's controls (the firm's controls, in ASSURANCE_MODEL.md and IRQ-RES-007 field 18), other than its own AI systems, to attack by external parties — including its identity, authentication and payment-authorisation controls — whether or not the attack uses AI. The out-of-scope limb of capability-envelope note CEN-1 (REGISTER_STATS.md), which applies the exclusion to one event, carries the same boundary: the resistance of the firm's controls, other than its own AI systems, to attack by external parties using that capability, including its identity, authentication and payment-authorisation controls. The own-AI boundary is stated because the Security domain (IRQ-SEC) tests the resistance of the institution's own AI systems to attack; TAXONOMY.md adds that an AI system that itself performs an identity, authentication, fraud-control or payment-authorisation function is one of the institution's own AI systems and is in scope (IRQ-SEC-013). The substance of the exclusion is unchanged; its pointer to the threat-led testing regimes is corrected in TAXONOMY.md (the next entry). Basis: the scope decision recorded at v1.0.3, whose wording, not the decision, was widened by the owner's ruling of 19 September 2026, with the carve-out for the institution's own AI systems confirmed by the owner's ruling of 25 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>TAXONOMY.md, the scope exclusion's pointer to threat-led testing regimes.</strong> STAR-FS was attributed to the ECB together with TIBER-EU. STAR-FS is the UK financial authorities' framework; the pointer now names each regime's owner. Basis: Bank of England, <em>STAR-FS Implementation Guide</em>: \"This STAR-FS Implementation Guide has been developed by the UK Financial Authorities (Bank of England Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA) and Financial Conduct Authority (FCA), and CREST.\"; the ECB's TIBER-EU page (developed by the ECB and the EU's national central banks, published May 2018, updated in 2024 for alignment with the DORA regulatory technical standards on threat-led penetration testing).</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"4. Register statistics","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>REGISTER_STATS.md, four-test dispositions, IRQ-GOV-014 (Test 2, evidenced harm mechanism).</strong> The row gave as the evidence that documented AI failures surfaced externally, not by the firm first: a characterisation of how AI failures come to light, for which the row cites no source and on which the canvas's own four-test validation does not rest. The row now gives the basis that validation states — DORA Art. 17's categorisation requirement and Art. 19's notification structure, and FCA SUP 15.3.1R's \"becomes aware\" trigger, all of which presuppose that the event is first identified and logged as an incident — and, from the canvas's research basis, that the control area is regulatory-instrument-led rather than research-led. ★2 unchanged. Basis: IRQ-GOV-014's canvas, fields 18 and 21. Primaries: Regulation (EU) 2022/2554, Art. 17(3)(b) and Art. 19(1); FCA Handbook, SUP 15.3.1R.</li>\n<li><strong>REGISTER_STATS.md, four-test dispositions, IRQ-CON-009 (Test 2, evidenced harm mechanism).</strong> The row gave as the evidence documented FS chatbot failures in ordinary use, citing the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau's issue spotlight of 6 June 2023. The canvas does not cite the spotlight, and its research basis records that no published FS-specific incident report confirming an AI system produced harmful, abusive or degrading output to a customer under genuinely non-adversarial conditions was located. The spotlight describes chatbots failing to recognise and resolve disputes, giving inaccurate information and hindering access to human intervention; it describes no harmful, abusive or degrading output. The row now gives the basis the canvas's four-test validation states — AILuminate's hazard-taxonomy framework (Ghosh et al., 2025), the closest available measurement approach for a currently untested failure surface — and the research basis's record that no such report was located, an absence consistent with an untested surface and not evidence of low risk. ★2 unchanged. Basis: IRQ-CON-009's canvas, fields 18 and 21. Primary: Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, <em>Chatbots in consumer finance</em>, issue spotlight, 6 June 2023.</li>\n<li><strong>REGISTER_STATS.md, four-test dispositions, IRQ-AGT-014 (Test 2, evidenced harm mechanism).</strong> The row gave \"~10 documented events Apr–Aug 2026 across four labs and two evaluators\". The canvas's four-test validation at admission enumerated ten events, occurring February–August 2026, across three frontier labs, one national institute and one independent third-party evaluator; the approximation, the months and the grouping were not the canvas's. At v1.2.0 the validation was re-stated as twelve events, the record spanning January–September 2026, with an external testing partner's own sandbox added; at v1.2.1 it counts eleven, one of the twelve excluded as a red-teaming exercise the evaluator set against its own production infrastructure (§2), and the Meta instance rests on Meta's own account of 14 August 2026 and on press reporting. The row now gives the admission record as the canvas stated it and notes the v1.2.0 and v1.2.1 enumerations and the Meta instance's sources at v1.2.1. ★2 unchanged. Basis: IRQ-AGT-014's canvas, field 21, as signed at v1.1.0, as re-stated at v1.2.0 and as corrected at v1.2.1.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"5. Working vocabulary","html":"<p>Text signed at v1.2.0 and earlier, and some text new in v1.2.1, carried vocabulary from the way the standard was produced: labels for how a check or search was run; references to the working period in which a note was written, to working records, to the working environment and to item or rule numbers on working lists; directions addressed to the time of signing; and references to the owner's decisions and rulings by record number. A reader of the standard cannot consult any of them. Each is replaced with what it stood for, dated where the note's own bracket or the tree gives the date, or removed where the sentence stands without it. No validation, verification status, source, rating, scope or finding changes, except in the one note on the Digital Services Act entry named below. Basis: the owner's rulings of 25 September 2026, which extend the change to change-log entries and other text kept as history (the entry on lines kept as history, below). These changes are recorded here, by kind: the change logs and document-control blocks of the canvases and playbooks do not record them, and no research-basis stamp moves for them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Validation and reviewer lines.</strong> CANVASES_CON.md, CANVASES_RES.md and CANVASES_TPR.md, 23 four-test validation lines; QA_LOG_BATCH1–3, the reviewer lines; the validation lines of IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-CON-009, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-MOD-016 and IRQ-TPR-009. These lines qualified the quality-assurance review, the blind audits or the verification with working labels for how they were run, or pointed to a working record. They now name the review and its log, or the audit or verification and its date, without a working label or a pointer to a working record.</li>\n<li><strong>SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md, verification notes and the Summary.</strong> Where a note referred to the working period, to how a page was fetched, searched, summarised or drafted, to a working record, to the working environment or to a rule number of the working conventions, it now says what was done, with the date or the version where the reference was to a time. It gives its own verification date (29 or 31 July 2026 or 24 August 2026) or the date the row was re-read (12 September 2026). It names secondary material and a summary of a fetched page as such, and it names the copy relied on as the owner's retained copy. The Summary names the additions of 1, 25 and 26 August 2026 by date. Of the six directions addressed to the time of signing, two now apply before external reliance, one states what its verification tag rests on and two are removed. The sixth is discharged: Art 93 of Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 was read at the Publications Office on 25 September 2026, and the note now gives the dates it states, its verification tag recording that read beside the 31 July 2026 verification.</li>\n<li><strong>Working records, working lists and working periods, elsewhere.</strong> Canvases in the AGT, CON, GOV, MOD, RES and SEC domains; QA_LOG_BATCH1–2; INCIDENT_REGISTER.md; SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md; CLAIMS_REGISTER.md (the CLM-28 derivation and its footnote); REGISTER_STATS.md; PLAYBOOK_CON.md (the CON domain summary); PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8; ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §6A. A pointer to a batch's working record is removed, or replaced by the version or the dated additions it refers to. An item or rule number from a working list, an audit or the working conventions is removed or replaced by what it stood for, and an open adoption item becomes a pending adoption decision. The name and rule identifier of the check that enforces a resolve-by bound become \"the check\", or are dropped where the sentence already says the bound is enforced mechanically. A working label for how a search or review was run becomes plain wording, and a reference to the working period, to the project in which the standard was produced or to the standard's drafters gives the version or date or refers to the standard. A direction addressed to the time of signing in IRQ-AGT-014's regulatory basis now applies before external reliance.</li>\n<li><strong>References to the owner's decisions and rulings by record number or working label.</strong> ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md (the review confidence statement); ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §6A.6; CLAIMS_REGISTER.md (the criticality-derivation label of all 28 claims, three reclassification sentences, the illegal-limb construction and the Summary); REGISTER_STATS.md; SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md; the incident register's inclusion note of 12 September 2026; IRQ-MOD-016 field 11; IRQ-RES-007 field 18 and its v1.2.1 change-log entry; TP-MOD-016; and §3 of this log (two). Each now states the decision or ruling and its date (31 July, 1 August, 25 August, 11 September or 12 September 2026), the version at which it is recorded (the scope decision, recorded at v1.0.3), or the construction it named.</li>\n<li><strong>Lines kept as history.</strong> The same replacements are made in the change-log entries dated before v1.2.1 of IRQ-AGT-008, IRQ-AGT-012, IRQ-AGT-014, IRQ-CON-001, IRQ-CON-003, IRQ-CON-004, IRQ-CON-009, IRQ-CON-010, IRQ-MOD-014, IRQ-MOD-015, IRQ-MOD-016, IRQ-SEC-004, IRQ-SEC-013, IRQ-SEC-014 and IRQ-TPR-009; in REGISTER_STATS.md's retained blocks as at v1.1.0 and v1.2.0 and its record of URO-1's resolution of 12 September 2026; in the incident register's note of 27 August 2026; in the addendum of 25 August 2026 to the correction log of 29 July 2026; and in the closing line of the correction log of 1 August 2026. No event, date or rating that these lines record changes.</li>\n<li><strong>The ownership proof, outside the manifest.</strong> ownership/OWNERSHIP_PROOF.md, which the manifest does not cover, carries the same kinds of change. In its v1.1.1 row the five added incident-register rows are recorded as added on the owner's instruction, in place of a ruling number, and the closure verification is named without its working structure; in its v1.2.0 row the two blind audits are named without a label for how each was run; the housekeeping line of 31 July 2026 no longer describes the working environment. Nothing these lines record changes, and the signed v1.1.1 and v1.2.0 attestations are unchanged. Completed on 26 September 2026, on the owner's ruling of 26 September 2026 and the file's own note of that date: the re-signed line names the re-signings of 30 and 31 July 2026; the v1.0.3 signing is dated 31 July 2026 wherever the file dates it, the date its archived attestation bears recorded beside it; the archived-signings clause is bounded to the six superseded versioned signings; the Bitcoin-attestation headline is bounded to the signings from v1.0.1 to v1.2.0; and the footer's revision dates are completed. The v1.2.1 row's re-check dates were extended at each later re-check, the last on 28 September 2026.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"6. Development record and build dates","html":"<p>Made on 28 September 2026 under the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026. No validation, verification status, source, rating, scope or finding changes. The changes are recorded here by kind; the files' change logs and document-control blocks do not record them, and no research-basis stamp moves for them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md.</strong> The document records the design philosophy, the design decisions in sequence, the four-test inclusion discipline and the maintenance model. Its sections on how the first build's content was produced and on that build's limitations are removed, with the dates of the build and the sentence presenting the document as a provenance record: they described the method of the first build, which has since changed. The parallel design drafts it describes as assessed critically are described as including output from AI systems, and the maintenance model is renumbered §4. Basis: the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>Dates of the first build.</strong> The dates the text carried from the build that preceded the first signing of 30 July 2026 are given to the month (2026-07, July 2026): change-log lines; the compiled, last-updated and date stamps of document-control blocks; the dates on validation lines; the headings of the two verification logs; in IRQ-MOD-016's change log, the date since which a deliberate gap had stood unexamined, with its restatement in REGISTER_STATS.md; and the regulatory register's date for the counts of its first compilation, with its restatement. The standard's rules for change logs and validation lines require a date (METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md; IRQ_TEMPLATE.md), and a month meets them. Dates of verification, access and sources, dates of events, dates from the first signing onward, the correction logs of 29 July and 1 August 2026, the dates this log records the text as having carried, and the signed attestations are unchanged. Basis: the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>A working title for the owner.</strong> Four passages that recorded what the owner did under a working title (\"sponsor\") no longer use it: in REGISTER_STATS.md, IRQ-GOV-012's admission record and the disposition of the concept-drift candidate merged into IRQ-MOD-010; IRQ-GOV-012's place in the GOV canvases' preamble; and the v1.0 change-log line of the IRQ-MOD-006 exemplar. Basis: the owner's rulings of 27 September 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>The archived project plan, outside the manifest.</strong> 00_Admin/ARCHIVE/PROJECT_PLAN.md's two banners no longer refer to DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md as the account of how the standard was built, and no longer date the build. The plan itself is unchanged.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<p><em>All corrections verified on disk before signing. Signed into the v1.2.1 manifest.</em></p>"}]};
